INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `department_id`, `subject_id`, `question_text`, `explanation`, `created_at`) VALUES
(1, 1, 2, 'A social media platform aims to forecast whether a user will engage with an advertisement. With access to user demographic information and historical advertisement interaction data, they choose to employ Logistic Regression algorithm for the prediction task. What is the primary objective of the social media company in this scenario?', 'Logistic Regression is a supervised learning algorithm used for binary classification. Here, the goal is to predict a binary outcome: will the user engage (click/interact) or not? It models the probability of engagement based on features (demographics, past behavior). Forecasting demographics is not the goal. Predicting past behavior is for training. Clustering is unsupervised. Understanding machine learning objectives and algorithm selection is critical for data science applications in marketing, finance, healthcare, and other domains where predictive modeling drives decisions.', '2026-02-26 10:47:54'),
(2, 1, 2, 'What will be output of the following Java program? class exception_handling { public static void main(String args[]) { try { int a = args.length; int b = 10 / a; System.out.print(a); } catch (ArithmeticException e) { System.out.println(\"1\"); } } }', 'If no command-line arguments are provided, args.length is 0. Then 10 / a causes division by zero, throwing ArithmeticException. The catch block catches it and prints \"1\". If arguments are provided, a > 0, division succeeds, and a (number of arguments) is printed. This demonstrates exception handling: try-catch blocks handle runtime errors gracefully. Understanding exception flow is crucial for writing robust Java programs that handle unexpected conditions without crashing. This includes knowing common exceptions, checked vs. unchecked exceptions, and proper error handling strategies in software development.', '2026-02-26 19:34:09'),
(3, 1, 2, 'Which of the following handles exception in Java when a catch is not used?', 'If an exception is not caught by any catch block in the call stack, the Java Runtime Environment\'s default exception handler catches it, prints the stack trace, and terminates the program. This is the last line of defense. finally blocks execute regardless but don\'t handle the exception. throw is for throwing exceptions. There\'s no \'default handle\' keyword. Understanding exception propagation is essential for debugging and designing exception hierarchies. Uncaught exceptions in production can cause application crashes, so proper exception handling (catch, logging, recovery) is a critical aspect of reliable software development in Java.', '2026-02-26 19:40:57'),
(4, 1, 2, 'What service allows users to access files stored on a central server from their local computers?', 'FTP is a standard network protocol for transferring files between a client and server over a TCP network. It allows users to upload, download, and manage files on a remote server. While insecure (plaintext), secure variants like SFTP and FTPS exist. Email is for messaging. RDP provides remote GUI access. DNS resolves names. FTP is widely used for website deployment, data exchange, and automated backups. Understanding FTP and its alternatives (SCP, Rsync) is important for system administration, web development, and data management tasks in networked environments.', '2026-02-27 13:02:34'),
(5, 1, 2, 'Which type of vulnerability occurs when software developers unintentionally expose sensitive information in error messages?', 'Information disclosure vulnerabilities occur when an application reveals sensitive data such as system details, database schemas, file paths, or user information through error messages, logs, or responses. This information can aid attackers in further exploits. For example, SQL error messages might reveal database structure. Prevention includes generic error messages in production, proper logging without sensitive data, and input validation. This differs from buffer overflow (memory corruption), XSS (client-side scripting), or privilege escalation (rights elevation). Information disclosure is part of the CIA triad (Confidentiality) and is addressed through secure coding, configuration management, and security testing.', '2026-02-27 13:05:27'),
(6, 1, 2, 'The recurrence relation for the Merge Sort algorithm is', 'Merge Sort is a divide-and-conquer algorithm that recursively splits the array into two halves (2T(n/2)), sorts each, and merges them in O(n) time. The recurrence relation T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n) captures this structure. Solving it via the Master Theorem yields O(n log n) time complexity in all cases (best, average, worst). This efficiency makes Merge Sort suitable for large datasets, though it requires O(n) auxiliary space. Understanding recurrences is key to analyzing recursive algorithms, comparing sorting methods, and predicting performance. Merge Sort\'s stability and predictable performance make it a foundational algorithm in computer science.', '2026-02-27 13:09:00'),
(8, 1, 2, 'What is the purpose of a function prototype in C++?', 'A function prototype (declaration) informs the compiler about a function\'s name, return type, and parameters before its full definition. This allows functions to be called before they are defined and enables separate compilation. The definition includes the body. Prototypes are essential for code organization, header files, and managing dependencies in large C++ projects. Understanding function declarations vs. definitions is fundamental to modular programming and avoiding compilation errors related to undefined functions.', '2026-02-27 13:12:43'),
(9, 1, 2, 'Which register is responsible to facilitate the communication between CPU and memory?', 'The Memory Address Register (MAR) holds the address of the memory location to be accessed. The Memory Data Register (MDR) holds the data being read from or written to memory. Together, they facilitate CPU-memory communication during fetch and execute cycles. The Program Counter (PC) holds the address of the next instruction. Understanding CPU registers is fundamental to computer organization, instruction execution, and the fetch-decode-execute cycle. These registers are part of the processor\'s interface with the memory hierarchy.', '2026-02-27 13:13:53'),
(11, 1, 2, 'Consider a class hierarchy with interfaces and abstract classes. Which of the following statements is CORRECT about implementing polymorphism in this scenario?', 'In Java-like OOP, interfaces define method signatures (abstract by default) that implementing classes must provide. Abstract classes can have both abstract and concrete methods, allowing partial implementation. Polymorphism is achieved through inheritance (overriding) and interfaces, not just overloading (same name, different parameters). Interfaces promote loose coupling by defining contracts without implementation details. Concrete subclasses of abstract classes must implement abstract methods but may inherit concrete ones. Understanding these distinctions is key to designing flexible, maintainable object-oriented systems using abstraction, encapsulation, and polymorphism effectively.', '2026-02-27 14:59:16'),
(12, 1, 2, 'Which feature of OOP is indicated by the following code? Class Student { int marks; } class Topper extends Student { private int age; public Topper(int age) { this.age=age; } }', 'The code shows inheritance: Topper extends Student, meaning Topper inherits the marks field from Student. There\'s no polymorphism shown (no method overriding or interfaces). Encapsulation is partially present with private age field, but the question focuses on the relationship between classes. Inheritance allows code reuse and hierarchy creation. Understanding inheritance is fundamental to OOP, enabling the \'is-a\' relationship, specialization, and polymorphic behavior when combined with method overriding. This concept is applied in frameworks, libraries, and domain modeling across programming languages like Java, C++, and Python.', '2026-02-27 15:03:43'),
(13, 1, 2, 'Which of the following is NOT a common component of a security policy?', 'A security policy defines an organization\'s approach to protecting information assets. Common components include: acceptable use, access control, data encryption, incident response, user training, and compliance requirements. Hardware maintenance is an operational procedure, not typically a security policy component (though security policies may reference maintenance practices). Effective security policies are comprehensive, enforceable, and regularly reviewed. They form the foundation of an information security management system (ISMS) and are essential for risk management, regulatory compliance, and creating a security-aware culture within organizations.', '2026-02-27 15:05:05'),
(14, 1, 2, 'What will be the output of the following code snippet? #include <iostream> using namespace std; void printEvenNumbers(int arr[], int size) { for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) { if (arr[i] % 2 == 0) { cout << arr[i] << \" \"; } } } int main() { int numbers[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}; printEvenNumbers(numbers, 6); return 0; }', 'The function iterates through the array and prints elements that are even (divisible by 2). The even numbers in {1,2,3,4,5,6} are 2, 4, 6. This demonstrates basic array traversal and conditional logic in C++. Understanding loops and arrays is fundamental for processing collections of data, a common task in programming algorithms, data analysis, and application development.', '2026-02-27 15:07:23'),
(15, 1, 2, 'Which data structure is most efficient for implementing a priority queue?', 'A binary heap (especially a min-heap or max-heap) provides O(log n) for insert and extract-min/max operations, and O(1) for peek, making it the standard efficient implementation for a priority queue. Balanced BSTs also offer O(log n) but with more overhead. Hash tables don\'t support priority ordering. Linked lists would be O(n). Priority queues are used in algorithms like Dijkstra\'s, Huffman coding, and job scheduling. Understanding heap operations and properties is fundamental to algorithm design and implementation of efficient data management systems.', '2026-02-27 15:08:33'),
(16, 1, 2, 'What is the main difference between Agile and Waterfall development methodologies?', 'Agile methodologies (e.g., Scrum, XP) are iterative and incremental, with short development cycles (sprints), continuous feedback, and adaptability to changing requirements. Waterfall is sequential, with distinct phases (requirements, design, implementation, testing, maintenance) where each must be completed before the next begins. Agile emphasizes customer collaboration, working software, and responding to change, while Waterfall relies on upfront planning and documentation. Both can be used for various project sizes, though Agile suits dynamic environments, and Waterfall fits stable, well-understood requirements. The choice impacts project management, team structure, documentation, and risk handling in software engineering projects.', '2026-02-27 15:09:53'),
(17, 1, 2, 'You have been tasked with designing a database for a library system that will track books, borrowers, and borrowings. Which of the following database concepts should you apply?', 'Normalization is the process of organizing data to reduce redundancy and improve integrity by dividing tables and defining relationships. For a transactional library system, normalization (typically to 3NF) ensures efficient storage, avoids anomalies, and maintains consistency. OLTP is the system type but not a design concept. Big data and data warehousing are for analytics, not transactional systems. Understanding normalization is fundamental to relational database design, involving functional dependencies, normal forms, and trade-offs with denormalization for performance.', '2026-02-27 15:12:04'),
(18, 1, 2, 'Which of the following operations is used if we are interested in only certain columns of a table?', 'Projection (Π) in relational algebra selects specific columns from a table, reducing the vertical dimension. Selection (σ) filters rows. UNION combines rows from two tables. JOIN combines columns from related tables. Understanding these basic operations is fundamental to database querying and SQL. Projection corresponds to the SELECT clause in SQL (listing columns). Mastery of these operations enables efficient data retrieval and manipulation in relational databases.', '2026-02-27 15:13:42'),
(19, 1, 2, 'What is the primary function of memory management unit in computer system?', 'The Memory Management Unit (MMU) translates logical (virtual) addresses generated by the CPU into physical addresses in main memory. This enables virtual memory, protection, and memory isolation between processes. Caching address translations is done by the TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer), a part of the MMU. The MMU is fundamental to modern operating systems, allowing efficient and secure memory utilization through paging, segmentation, and memory protection mechanisms. Understanding MMU operation is key to system programming, OS design, and performance tuning.', '2026-02-27 15:18:46'),
(20, 1, 2, 'Which of the following is the most powerful type of machine?', 'The Turing machine is the most powerful computational model in the Chomsky hierarchy, capable of recognizing recursively enumerable languages (Type 0). DFAs and NFAs recognize regular languages (Type 3). Pushdown automata recognize context-free languages (Type 2). Turing machines serve as the theoretical foundation for modern computers and the study of computability and complexity (P vs NP, halting problem). Understanding the hierarchy of automata and their corresponding languages is fundamental to theoretical computer science, compiler construction, and understanding the limits of algorithmic computation.', '2026-02-27 15:23:54'),
(21, 1, 2, 'What role does a type system play in the type-checking process?', 'A type system assigns types to expressions, variables, and functions, and enforces type rules (e.g., no adding a string to an integer). Type checking, performed during semantic analysis, ensures program correctness before execution. Syntax rules are defined by grammar. Tokens are handled by the lexer. Code generation is a later phase. Understanding type systems is crucial for language design, compiler construction, and writing type-safe programs that prevent common errors and improve reliability.', '2026-02-27 15:30:16'),
(22, 1, 2, 'Which of the following sorting algorithm is of divide-and-conquer type?', 'Quick sort is a classic divide-and-conquer algorithm: it picks a pivot, partitions the array into elements less than and greater than the pivot (divide), and recursively sorts the sub-arrays (conquer). Merge sort is another prime example. Bubble, insertion, and selection sorts are incremental (brute-force) approaches. Understanding algorithm design paradigms (divide-and-conquer, greedy, dynamic programming) is fundamental to computer science, enabling the design and analysis of efficient algorithms for sorting, searching, and optimization problems.', '2026-02-27 15:31:49'),
(23, 1, 2, 'For the linked list implementation of the queue, where are the enqueue and dequeues performed?', 'In a queue (FIFO), enqueue adds to the rear, dequeue removes from the front. In a linked list implementation, maintaining pointers to both head (front) and tail (rear) allows O(1) enqueue (add after tail) and O(1) dequeue (remove head). This efficient implementation is fundamental to queue data structures used in scheduling, buffering, and BFS algorithms. Understanding the underlying structure of abstract data types is key to implementing them correctly and efficiently in programming languages.', '2026-02-27 15:33:42'),
(24, 1, 2, 'You are creating a use case model for an online library system. Which of the following is the MOST appropriate use case description for the \"Borrow a Book\" functionality?', 'A proper use case description focuses on system behavior and actor interactions from a black-box perspective, detailing the flow of events to achieve a user goal. Option B correctly describes the system\'s responsibilities (verification, retrieval, update, notification) without delving into UI specifics or implementation details. Use cases should be technology-agnostic, emphasize system responses, and capture main success scenarios. They are foundational in requirements engineering, helping stakeholders understand functionality, supporting test case development, and guiding system design. Well-written use cases improve communication between users and developers and serve as a basis for system documentation', '2026-02-27 15:37:01'),
(25, 1, 2, 'Which of the following operating system functions contributes to the efficient utilization of system resources by preventing conflicts and bottlenecks?', 'Process management (scheduling, synchronization) and memory management (allocation, paging) are core OS functions that optimize resource usage, prevent conflicts (deadlocks, race conditions), and avoid bottlenecks (CPU starvation, memory thrashing). They ensure fair and efficient sharing of CPU, memory, and other resources among competing processes. Security protects resources. Device management controls I/O. IPC enables communication. Together, these functions allow the OS to provide a stable, performant multitasking environment. Understanding them is fundamental to OS design, performance tuning, and developing efficient applications.', '2026-02-27 15:43:34'),
(26, 1, 2, 'Which layer of the OSI model is responsible for converting data packets from the Network layer into electrical signals for transmission over the physical medium?', 'The Physical Layer (Layer 1) deals with the transmission and reception of raw bit streams over a physical medium. It defines electrical, mechanical, procedural, and functional specifications for activating, maintaining, and deactivating physical links. This includes cables, connectors, signaling, voltage levels, data rates, and modulation. It converts digital bits into signals (electrical, optical, radio) for transmission and vice versa. Higher layers handle logical addressing, routing, and end-to-end communication. Understanding the Physical Layer is crucial for network infrastructure, cabling standards, signal integrity, and technologies like Ethernet, USB, and Bluetooth. It\'s the foundation upon which all networking is built.', '2026-02-27 15:44:51'),
(27, 1, 2, 'Using Relational Algebra the query that finds customers, who have a balance of over 1000 is', 'In relational algebra, σ (sigma) is selection (filter rows), Π (pi) is projection (select columns). To find customers with balance > 1000: first select rows with σ_balance>1000(Deposit), then project customer names with Π_Customer_name. Option C correctly sequences these operations. Understanding relational algebra is foundational for database theory, query optimization, and understanding how SQL is executed. It provides a formal basis for relational database operations.', '2026-02-27 15:49:30'),
(28, 1, 2, 'What is the broadcast address in a subnet with the address range 192.168.1.0 - 192.168.1.31?', 'The range 192.168.1.0 to 192.168.1.31 is a /27 subnet (32 addresses). The first address (192.168.1.0) is the network address. The last address (192.168.1.31) is the broadcast address, used to send packets to all hosts in the subnet. Broadcast addresses are not assignable to hosts. Understanding broadcast addresses is essential for network configuration, troubleshooting, and security (broadcast storms, attacks). It\'s a key concept in IP addressing, subnetting, and network protocol behavior (e.g., ARP, DHCP). Misconfiguration can lead to communication failures or security vulnerabilities.', '2026-02-27 16:49:26'),
(29, 1, 2, 'Which of the following situations incorporates the application of reinforcement learning?', 'Reinforcement Learning (RL) involves an agent learning to make decisions by performing actions and receiving rewards/penalties from the environment. Training a robot in a maze is a classic RL problem: the agent (robot) learns a policy (route) to maximize cumulative reward (reach goal quickly). Chatbots often use supervised or NLP. Customer segmentation is clustering. Article grouping is clustering or classification. RL is key in robotics, game playing (AlphaGo), autonomous vehicles, and resource management where learning through interaction is necessary. Understanding RL paradigms is important for advanced AI applications.', '2026-02-27 16:57:39'),
(30, 1, 2, 'When evaluating the suitability of preemptive and non-preemptive scheduling algorithms for real-time systems, which of the following is the most important consideration?', 'Real-time systems require guaranteed response times and deadline meeting. Preemptive scheduling (e.g., Rate Monotonic, EDF) is typically used because it allows higher-priority tasks to interrupt lower-priority ones, ensuring critical deadlines are met. Predictability and determinism are more important than fairness, simplicity, or throughput. Hard real-time systems often use static priority preemptive scheduling. Understanding real-time scheduling is essential for embedded systems, robotics, avionics, and industrial control, where missed deadlines can have severe consequences. This involves analyzing worst-case execution times, priority inversion, and scheduling feasibility', '2026-02-27 17:03:04'),
(31, 1, 2, 'When creating requirements using use case modeling concepts, which of the following activities involves identifying the actors, their goals, and the interactions between them and the system?', 'Use case diagrams visually represent actors (external entities interacting with the system), use cases (system functionalities), and their relationships. This modeling activity helps identify system boundaries, actor roles, and functional requirements early in development. It supports requirements elicitation by focusing on user goals and system responses. Pseudocode, database design, and test development occur later. Use case diagrams are part of UML and serve as communication tools between stakeholders and developers, providing a high-level overview of system functionality without implementation details. They are often complemented by detailed use case descriptions and activity diagrams for comprehensive requirements specification.', '2026-02-27 20:04:00'),
(32, 1, 2, 'A company wants to create a database to track employee schedules, hours worked, and pay rates. They also want the database to calculate employee pay based on hours worked and pay rates. Which of the following database concepts should be applied to this database?', 'Stored procedures are precompiled SQL routines stored in the database that can encapsulate business logic, such as pay calculation. They improve performance, security, and maintainability by centralizing logic. Triggers are event-driven. Functions return values. Views are virtual tables. For complex calculations and business rules, stored procedures are appropriate. Understanding database programming objects is essential for implementing application logic within the database tier, optimizing performance, and ensuring data consistency in multi-tier architectures.', '2026-02-27 20:05:17'),
(33, 1, 2, 'Which of the following is a common method used by attackers to exploit buffer overflow vulnerabilities?', 'Buffer overflow occurs when more data is written to a buffer than it can hold, causing adjacent memory to be overwritten. Attackers exploit this by sending excessive input to overflow the buffer and overwrite critical data like return addresses, potentially injecting and executing malicious code. This is a common vulnerability in C/C++ programs lacking bounds checking. Defenses include secure coding practices (bounds checking, safe functions), compiler protections (stack canaries, ASLR), and non-executable memory (NX bit). Understanding buffer overflows is essential for secure software development, vulnerability assessment, and implementing runtime protections in operating systems and applications.', '2026-02-27 20:14:26'),
(34, 1, 2, 'Which sorting algorithm has the same average and worst-case time bounds (in Big-Oh) as heapsort?', 'Heapsort has O(n log n) time complexity in both average and worst cases. Merge sort also has O(n log n) in all cases. Insertion sort is O(n^2) average and worst. Quicksort is O(n log n) average but O(n^2) worst. Shell sort depends on gap sequence but is generally worse than O(n log n). Understanding the time complexity guarantees of sorting algorithms is crucial for selecting the right algorithm based on data characteristics and performance requirements. Merge sort and heapsort provide consistent performance, while quicksort is often faster in practice but with a risky worst case.', '2026-02-27 20:17:19'),
(35, 1, 2, 'Which principle ensures that critical systems remain operational in case of hardware failure?', 'Failover is the automatic switching to a redundant or standby system upon failure of the primary system, ensuring high availability and business continuity. It\'s a key disaster recovery strategy. Load balancing distributes workload across multiple systems to improve performance and reliability. Data normalization is a database design technique. DoS prevention protects against attacks. Failover mechanisms include clustering, RAID, redundant power supplies, and geographically distributed servers. Understanding failover is crucial for designing resilient IT infrastructure, minimizing downtime, and meeting service level agreements (SLAs) in enterprise and cloud environments.', '2026-02-27 20:37:47'),
(36, 1, 1, 'If you want to test individual sub-systems of software, which phase of software testing is appropriate?', 'Unit testing focuses on verifying individual components or modules in isolation, typically performed by developers on small units of code like functions, methods, or classes. Integration testing combines and tests multiple units together, system testing evaluates the complete integrated system, and acceptance testing validates against user requirements. Unit tests are fundamental for catching bugs early, enabling refactoring, and ensuring component reliability before integration. They\'re usually automated and run frequently during development.', '2026-02-27 20:41:45'),
(37, 1, 2, 'Which technique is used in dynamic programming to solve optimization problems?', 'Memoization is a DP technique that stores results of expensive function calls and returns cached results when the same inputs occur again. It\'s typically implemented with recursion and a lookup table (e.g., array, hash map). This avoids redundant computations in problems with overlapping sub-problems, like Fibonacci or edit distance. Tabulation is the bottom-up iterative alternative. Divide and Conquer doesn\'t store results. Greedy makes local choices without backtracking. Heuristics are approximate methods. Memoization is a key optimization in recursive algorithms, improving time complexity at the cost of extra space. It\'s widely used in competitive programming, algorithm design, and performance-critical applications.', '2026-02-27 20:46:53'),
(38, 1, 2, 'A software system experiences performance issues during peak usage times. How can you analyze the problem to identify the root cause?', 'Performance analysis involves profiling the system to identify bottlenecks—such as CPU-intensive functions, memory leaks, disk I/O, or network latency—under load. Tools like profilers, APM (Application Performance Monitoring), and logging help pinpoint inefficient code, database queries, or resource contention. Optimization may include algorithm improvement, caching, concurrency adjustments, or database indexing. UI changes or hardware upgrades without analysis are speculative and costly. Rewriting is extreme and risky. Performance engineering requires measurement before optimization, understanding scalability patterns, and designing for expected load. This is critical for user satisfaction and system reliability in production environments', '2026-02-27 20:48:45'),
(39, 1, 2, 'Which of the following is best describe the relationship between formal languages, formal grammars, and automata?', 'Formal languages are sets of strings over an alphabet. Formal grammars (e.g., Chomsky hierarchy) are sets of production rules that generate the strings of a language. Automata (e.g., finite automata, pushdown automata, Turing machines) are computational models that recognize (accept) strings belonging to a language. This correspondence between grammars (generators) and automata (recognizers) is central to formal language theory and compiler design. Understanding this relationship is key to parsing, language design, and the theoretical limits of computation.', '2026-02-27 21:20:15'),
(40, 1, 2, 'One of the following is server-side scripting', 'PHP is a server-side scripting language executed on the web server to generate dynamic HTML sent to the client. HTML and CSS are client-side markup and styling. JavaScript can be both client-side and (with Node.js) server-side, but traditionally it\'s client-side. Understanding the client-server model in web development is essential. Server-side scripting handles tasks like database access, authentication, and business logic, while client-side code manages user interface and interactions. This separation is key to scalable and secure web application architecture.', '2026-02-27 21:44:46'),
(41, 1, 2, 'Which of the following is an example of proactive troubleshooting?', 'Proactive troubleshooting involves continuous monitoring, trend analysis, and preventive measures to identify and resolve potential issues before they cause outages or degradation. This includes using monitoring tools (Nagios, Prometheus), log analysis, performance baselining, and predictive maintenance. Reactive approaches wait for failures. Proactive management improves system reliability, user satisfaction, and reduces downtime and costs. It\'s a core principle in IT service management (ITIL) and DevOps practices. Understanding proactive vs. reactive strategies is essential for effective system administration and operations in modern IT environments.', '2026-02-27 21:46:34'),
(42, 1, 2, 'Consider the grammar S → aS | b. Which of the following strings can be generated from S?', 'The grammar produces strings: start with S, replace S with aS any number of times, then finally replace S with b. This yields strings of zero or more \'a\'s followed by a single \'b\'. So language L = {a^n b | n ≥ 0}. \'aaab\' fits (three a\'s then b). \'abab\' has b in middle. \'aabb\' has two b\'s. \'bba\' starts with b. Understanding context-free grammars and derivation is fundamental to formal language theory, parsing, and compiler design, enabling the specification and recognition of language syntax.', '2026-02-27 21:47:58'),
(43, 1, 2, 'Which wireless technology is commonly used for local area networking and internet access within buildings?', 'Wi-Fi (IEEE 802.11) is the standard wireless LAN technology for indoor internet access and local networking. It provides high-speed connectivity over short to medium ranges using radio waves in 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands. Bluetooth is for short-range device pairing (PAN). Cellular provides wide-area mobile connectivity. NFC is for very short-range communication (contactless payments). Wi-Fi is integral to modern networking, supporting devices like laptops, smartphones, IoT devices, and smart home systems. Understanding Wi-Fi standards (802.11ac/ax), security (WPA3), and deployment (access points, mesh networks) is essential for network engineers and IT professionals.', '2026-02-27 21:50:24'),
(44, 1, 2, 'Which of the following is an example of a propositional logic statement?', 'Propositional logic deals with propositions (statements that are true or false) and their combinations using logical connectives (AND, OR, NOT, IMPLIES). \'A and B are both true\' is a compound proposition using AND. \'All cats are mammals\' is predicate logic (quantifiers). \'If it rains...\' is an implication but involves predicates. \'John is taller...\' is a relational statement. Understanding propositional logic is foundational for discrete mathematics, digital circuit design, and automated reasoning. It forms the basis for more complex logical systems used in computer science.', '2026-02-27 21:53:04'),
(45, 1, 2, 'Which type of attack involves an attacker intercepting and modifying communication between two parties?', 'Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks occur when an attacker secretly intercepts and potentially alters the communication between two parties who believe they are directly communicating with each other. This can happen in various network scenarios, including unsecured Wi-Fi, compromised routers, or through ARP spoofing. MitM attacks can lead to data theft, session hijacking, or injection of malicious content. Common defenses include encryption (TLS/SSL), digital certificates, and secure authentication protocols. Understanding MitM helps in implementing proper network security measures, such as VPNs, certificate pinning, and intrusion detection systems.', '2026-02-27 21:54:11'),
(46, 1, 2, 'Which of the following is NOT a role of a lexer in a compiler?', 'The lexer (lexical analyzer) scans source code, removes whitespace/comments, identifies tokens based on patterns, and reports lexical errors. Semantic analysis is performed later by the semantic analyzer, which checks type compatibility, scope, and other context-sensitive rules. Understanding the separation of concerns in compiler phases (lexical, syntax, semantic) is fundamental to compiler design. Each phase has distinct responsibilities, enabling modular construction and easier debugging of the compilation process.', '2026-02-27 21:56:29'),
(47, 1, 2, 'Which one of the following is commonly used by lexical analyzers to define patterns for tokens?', 'Lexical analyzers (lexers) use regular expressions to define token patterns (e.g., identifiers, numbers, keywords). These patterns are matched against the source code to produce a stream of tokens. Parse trees and ASTs are used in syntax analysis. Symbol tables track identifiers. Regular expressions provide a concise and powerful way to specify lexical rules. Understanding lexing is the first step in compiler design, enabling the transformation of raw text into structured tokens for further processing by the parser.', '2026-02-27 21:59:35'),
(48, 1, 2, 'Which of the following problems can be solved using the greedy algorithm approach?', 'The Fractional Knapsack problem can be solved optimally with a greedy strategy: select items based on highest value-to-weight ratio until capacity is filled. Greedy algorithms make locally optimal choices at each step, hoping for a global optimum. They work when the problem exhibits greedy choice property and optimal substructure. In contrast, 0/1 Knapsack requires DP. Longest Common Subsequence uses DP. Traveling Salesman is NP-hard, typically approached with heuristics. Matrix Chain Multiplication uses DP. Greedy algorithms are efficient for problems like Huffman coding, activity selection, and Minimum Spanning Tree (Prim\'s, Kruskal\'s). Understanding problem characteristics guides algorithm selection for efficiency and correctness.', '2026-02-27 22:06:17'),
(49, 1, 2, 'Which of the following functions of a contemporary operating system contributes most directly to its ability to evolve and adapt to changing requirements and technological advancements?', 'A modular design with hardware abstraction layers (HAL) allows an OS to be extended, updated, and ported to new hardware with minimal changes to the core kernel. This separation of concerns enables adaptation to new technologies, drivers, and requirements. GUI is a user interface feature. Process/memory management are core but not primarily evolutionary. Software management is a higher-level service. Modularity and abstraction are key software engineering principles that make operating systems like Linux and Windows adaptable over decades. Understanding OS architecture is essential for system programming, driver development, and comprehending how OSes support diverse hardware and evolving software ecosystems.', '2026-03-02 11:58:03'),
(50, 1, 2, 'When designing complex software systems with multiple interacting objects, what design principle is crucial for maintainability and scalability?', 'Loose coupling minimizes dependencies between components, allowing changes in one part with minimal impact on others. This is achieved through interfaces, dependency injection, and design patterns. It improves maintainability, testability, and scalability. Tight coupling and global variables create hidden dependencies and side effects, making systems brittle. Code duplication violates DRY (Don\'t Repeat Yourself). Understanding coupling and cohesion is fundamental to software architecture, enabling modular, reusable, and adaptable designs. Principles like SOLID, design patterns, and modular programming promote loose coupling in large-scale software development.', '2026-03-02 12:05:09'),
(51, 1, 2, 'What network service provides centralized authentication and authorization for users and devices?', 'LDAP (Lightweight Directory Access Protocol) is used for accessing and managing distributed directory information services, commonly for centralized authentication and authorization (e.g., Microsoft Active Directory, OpenLDAP). It allows single sign-on (SSO) and centralized user management. DNS resolves domain names. DHCP assigns IP addresses. SNMP monitors network devices. LDAP is foundational in enterprise IT for managing users, groups, permissions, and resources across networks. Understanding LDAP is essential for system administration, security, and integrating applications with directory services.', '2026-03-02 12:08:04'),
(52, 1, 2, 'Which of the following is the primary reason why the Hold and Wait condition can lead to deadlock?', 'Hold and Wait is one of the four necessary conditions for deadlock: a process holds at least one resource while waiting for additional resources held by other processes. This circular dependency can lead to a stalemate where no process can proceed. Deadlock requires all four conditions: Mutual Exclusion, Hold and Wait, No Preemption, and Circular Wait. Prevention strategies target breaking these conditions. Understanding deadlock is crucial for designing concurrent systems, databases, and OS resource management. Techniques include resource ordering, timeouts, and deadlock detection/recovery algorithms.', '2026-03-02 12:10:30'),
(53, 1, 2, 'Which of the following is the first step in database design?', 'Database design begins with requirements definition: understanding user needs, data to be stored, and system functionality. This informs subsequent steps: conceptual design (ER diagrams), logical design (relational schema), and physical design (indexes, storage). Choosing a DBMS may happen early but depends on requirements. A systematic approach ensures the database supports applications effectively. Understanding the database design lifecycle is crucial for database administrators and developers to create efficient, scalable, and maintainable databases that meet business objectives.', '2026-03-02 12:11:23'),
(54, 1, 2, 'If the function T (n) = Ω(g(n), which statements are true?', 'Big-Omega (Ω) notation describes an asymptotic lower bound. T(n) = Ω(g(n)) means there exist positive constants c and n0 such that 0 ≤ c g(n) ≤ T(n) for all n ≥ n0. In other words, T(n) is at least a constant multiple of g(n) for sufficiently large n. Big-O gives an upper bound (T(n) ≤ c g(n)). Theta (Θ) gives tight bound (both upper and lower). Understanding asymptotic notations is crucial for analyzing algorithm efficiency, comparing growth rates, and determining worst-case, best-case, and average-case complexities in computer science.', '2026-03-02 12:13:42'),
(55, 1, 2, 'Which of the following is the primary advantage of non-preemptive scheduling algorithms over preemptive algorithms?', 'Non-preemptive scheduling (e.g., FCFS, SJF non-preemptive) allows a process to run until it voluntarily yields (blocks or finishes). This simplicity reduces context-switch overhead and synchronization complexity. However, it can cause starvation and poor responsiveness. Preemptive scheduling (e.g., Round Robin, priority preemptive) allows OS to interrupt processes, improving fairness and responsiveness but with increased overhead and complexity. Choice depends on system goals: non-preemptive for batch systems, preemptive for interactive/time-sharing systems. Understanding scheduling algorithms is key for OS design, real-time systems, and performance optimization in computing environments.', '2026-03-02 12:15:37'),
(56, 1, 2, 'What is the primary function of the ARP (Address Resolution Protocol)?', 'ARP maps an IP address (Layer 3) to a MAC address (Layer 2) within a local network. When a device needs to send a packet, it checks its ARP cache; if no entry exists, it broadcasts an ARP request asking \"Who has this IP?\" The owner replies with its MAC address. This resolution is essential for Ethernet and Wi-Fi communication. DNS translates domain names to IPs. Routing is handled by IP and routing protocols. Secure connections are managed by TLS/SSL. ARP is fundamental to LAN operation but also vulnerable to spoofing attacks (ARP poisoning). Understanding ARP is key for network troubleshooting, security, and understanding packet delivery.', '2026-03-02 12:16:57'),
(57, 1, 2, 'A company wants to track employee performance by department and job title. Which SQL operation would be used to accomplish this task?', 'GROUP BY aggregates data by specified columns (e.g., department, job_title) allowing calculations like averages, counts, or sums per group. Combined with aggregate functions (COUNT, AVG, SUM), it enables performance analysis. SELECT retrieves columns. FROM specifies tables. JOIN combines tables. GROUP BY is essential for summary reports and analytical queries in SQL. Understanding aggregation and grouping is crucial for business intelligence, data analysis, and generating insights from relational databases.', '2026-03-02 12:17:40'),
(58, 1, 2, 'What should be the output of the code below? #include<iostream> using namespace std; int main() { int a=5; int b=6; int c; c= (a>b) ? a : b; cout<<c; return 0; }', 'The ternary operator (condition) ? expr1 : expr2 evaluates to expr1 if condition is true, else expr2. Here, a>b is false (5>6), so c = b, which is 6. This demonstrates conditional expressions in C++. Understanding operators and control flow is fundamental to programming. The ternary operator is a concise alternative to if-else for simple assignments. Mastery of such constructs enables writing efficient and readable code in C++ and similar languages.', '2026-03-02 12:18:28'),
(59, 1, 2, 'Which of the following is a characteristic of a hardwired control unit?', 'Hardwired control units use fixed logic circuits (combinational and sequential) to generate control signals. They are faster than microprogrammed units because signals are generated directly by hardware. However, they are inflexible (difficult to modify) and have complex design for complex instruction sets. Microprogrammed control uses a stored program (microcode) for flexibility but is slower. Understanding control unit design is important for computer architecture, impacting processor performance, complexity, and adaptability to new instructions.', '2026-03-02 12:21:11'),
(60, 1, 2, 'Which loop structure is suitable when the number of iterations is known in advance?', 'The for loop is ideal when the number of iterations is known, as it combines initialization, condition, and update in one statement: for (int i=0; i<n; i++). while and do-while are better for unknown iterations based on a condition. switch is for multi-way branching, not looping. Understanding loop constructs enables choosing the right control flow for tasks like iterating over arrays, collections, or fixed-count processes. This is a basic but critical skill in algorithmic implementation across programming languages.', '2026-03-02 12:21:59'),
(61, 1, 2, 'Which of the following handles exception in Java when a catch is not used?', 'If an exception is not caught by any catch block in the call stack, the Java Runtime Environment\'s default exception handler catches it, prints the stack trace, and terminates the program. This is the last line of defense. finally blocks execute regardless but don\'t handle the exception. throw is for throwing exceptions. There\'s no \'default handle\' keyword. Understanding exception propagation is essential for debugging and designing exception hierarchies. Uncaught exceptions in production can cause application crashes, so proper exception handling (catch, logging, recovery) is a critical aspect of reliable software development in Java.', '2026-03-02 12:22:44'),
(62, 1, 2, 'You are analyzing user requirements for a new e-commerce platform. Which of the following is the most relevant technique to gather user feedback?', 'User interviews are a direct, qualitative method to understand user needs, behaviors, and pain points. They provide rich insights that surveys or analytics cannot capture. In requirements engineering, techniques like interviews, workshops, and observation help elicit authentic requirements, especially for user-centric systems like e-commerce. Code review, UI analysis, or assumption-based development are insufficient for gathering genuine user feedback. Effective requirements gathering combines multiple techniques (interviews, surveys, prototyping) to ensure the system meets real user needs, reduces rework, and increases adoption. This human-centered approach is critical in Agile and user experience (UX) design processes.', '2026-03-02 12:23:36'),
(63, 1, 2, 'Which cryptographic technique is used to ensure the integrity and authenticity of a message?', 'Digital signatures use asymmetric cryptography to provide integrity, authenticity, and non-repudiation. The sender signs the message with their private key, and the receiver verifies it with the sender\'s public key. This ensures the message hasn\'t been altered (integrity) and confirms the sender\'s identity (authenticity). Unlike encryption (confidentiality) or hashing (integrity only), digital signatures combine hashing with public-key cryptography. Steganography hides messages within other files. Digital signatures are foundational in secure communication, digital certificates, and software distribution, forming the basis of trust in many online transactions and authentication systems.', '2026-03-02 12:24:21'),
(64, 1, 2, 'Precedence graphs help to find a.', 'Precedence graphs (serialization graphs) test for conflict serializability in database transaction scheduling. If the graph has no cycles, the schedule is serializable. Serializability ensures correctness in concurrent transaction execution. Recoverability and cascade-less are related to transaction recovery. Deadlock is a different concurrency issue. Understanding serializability and precedence graphs is fundamental to database concurrency control, ensuring ACID properties (Isolation) in multi-user database systems.', '2026-03-02 12:25:14'),
(65, 1, 2, 'How many host addresses are available in a subnet with a subnet mask of 255.255.255.248 (CIDR notation /29)?', 'A /29 subnet mask (255.255.255.248) has 29 network bits and 3 host bits (32-29=3). Total addresses = 2^3 = 8. Subtract 2 for network and broadcast addresses (first and last) yields 6 usable host addresses. Subnetting is crucial for efficient IP allocation, network segmentation, and security. Understanding CIDR notation, binary conversion, and address calculation is fundamental in network design and administration. This skill applies to configuring routers, firewalls, and DHCP, as well as planning scalable and secure network architectures in both IPv4 and IPv6 contexts.', '2026-03-02 12:26:12'),
(66, 1, 2, 'Which of the following principle of cyber security restricts how privileges are initiated whenever any object or subject accesses a resource?', 'Complete Mediation requires that every access to every resource must be checked for authorization. This principle ensures that security policies are consistently enforced and prevents cached or bypassed access checks. It means the system should mediate all accesses, not just initial ones, to handle changes in permissions or revocation. Least Privilege limits users to minimum necessary access. Fail-Safe Defaults denies access by default. Economy of Mechanism favors simple designs. Complete Mediation is implemented through access control lists, capability systems, and security kernels, forming a core defense against privilege escalation and unauthorized resource access in secure operating systems and applications.', '2026-03-02 12:26:56'),
(67, 1, 2, 'After implementing a new software update, you notice an increase in user complaints about bugs. What is the MOST appropriate course of action?', 'A systematic approach to post-deployment issues involves triaging complaints, reproducing bugs, identifying root causes, and deploying targeted fixes (hotfixes). This minimizes user disruption and maintains trust. Reverting should be a last resort if the system is critically broken. Ignoring complaints damages user satisfaction. Adding new functionalities without fixing existing issues compounds problems. This process is part of software maintenance and requires effective logging, monitoring, and user feedback channels. It aligns with ITIL incident and problem management practices, emphasizing rapid response, transparency, and continuous improvement in software delivery and operations.', '2026-03-02 12:28:13'),
(68, 1, 2, 'Which authentication factor requires something the user knows?', 'Authentication factors are categorized into: knowledge (something you know – passwords, PINs), possession (something you have – tokens, smart cards), inherence (something you are – biometrics like fingerprints), and sometimes behavior (something you do – typing patterns). \'Something you know\' is the most common but also vulnerable to attacks like phishing, brute force, or social engineering. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) combines multiple factors for enhanced security. Understanding authentication factors is crucial for designing secure systems, selecting appropriate authentication methods, and implementing defense-in-depth strategies to protect against credential theft and unauthorized access.', '2026-03-02 12:28:56'),
(69, 1, 2, 'The main idea behind the divide-and-conquer algorithm is to:', 'Divide and Conquer involves three steps: divide the problem into smaller sub-problems, conquer (solve) them recursively, and combine their solutions to form the overall solution. This paradigm is efficient for problems where sub-problems are independent, such as Merge Sort, Quick Sort, and binary search. It differs from DP, which deals with overlapping sub-problems, and greedy, which makes incremental choices. Understanding D&C is fundamental for algorithm design, recurrence analysis, and parallel computing (since sub-problems can often be solved concurrently). Mastery of this approach enables solving complex problems efficiently through decomposition and recursion.', '2026-03-02 12:29:43'),
(70, 1, 2, 'Which file system is commonly used in windows operating system?', 'NTFS (New Technology File System) is the default file system for modern Windows versions (NT, 2000, XP, 7, 8, 10, 11). It supports large files, encryption, compression, journaling, and security permissions. FAT32 is older, with file size and partition limits. HFS+ is for macOS. Ext4 is for Linux. exFAT is for flash drives. NTFS is crucial for Windows administration, disk management, and data recovery. Understanding file system characteristics helps in choosing the right format for performance, compatibility, and features like quotas, shadow copies, and RAID support in Windows environments', '2026-03-02 12:30:50'),
(71, 1, 2, 'Which iptables rule is used to allow outgoing HTTP connections?', 'The correct iptables syntax to allow outgoing HTTP (port 80/TCP) is: iptables -A OUTPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT. This appends a rule to the OUTPUT chain for TCP protocol, destination port 80, with ACCEPT action. iptables uses numeric ports and explicit protocol specifications. Understanding iptables is crucial for Linux firewall configuration, network security, and controlling traffic flow. Mastery of rule syntax, chains (INPUT, OUTPUT, FORWARD), and targets (ACCEPT, DROP, REJECT) enables effective network filtering, NAT, and security policy enforcement on Linux systems.', '2026-03-02 12:31:37'),
(72, 1, 2, 'Which of the following is true about the dynamic programming approach?', 'Dynamic programming (DP) optimizes by breaking problems into overlapping sub-problems, solving each once, and storing results (memoization or tabulation) to avoid redundant computations. This is its defining characteristic. DP can be implemented iteratively or recursively. It\'s not always more efficient than greedy algorithms—greedy methods are faster but require optimal substructure and greedy choice property. DP applies to both optimization and counting problems (e.g., Fibonacci, shortest path). Key DP concepts include optimal substructure and overlapping sub-problems, used in algorithms like Floyd-Warshall, Knapsack, and sequence alignment. Mastery of DP is essential for algorithmic problem-solving and efficient computation.', '2026-03-02 12:32:40'),
(73, 1, 2, 'Which principle of compiler design ensures that the generated code runs efficiently on the target platform?', 'Code optimization transforms intermediate code to improve performance, reduce size, or lower power consumption without changing program semantics. It includes techniques like constant folding, dead code elimination, loop optimization, and register allocation. Lexical, syntax, and semantic analysis are earlier phases focused on correctness. Optimization is critical for producing efficient executables. Understanding compiler optimizations is important for both compiler writers and programmers to write code that compiles efficiently and to understand performance characteristics of generated code.', '2026-03-02 12:33:21'),
(74, 1, 2, 'Which of the following statement is INCORRECT about the Final key word in Java?', 'The incorrect statement is that a final method cannot be inherited. final methods CAN be inherited; they simply cannot be overridden by subclasses. final variables are constants. final classes cannot be extended (no subclasses). final is used to enforce immutability, prevent modification, and improve security/design. Understanding final is important for Java programming, especially when designing APIs, immutable objects, and ensuring method contracts. It\'s a key keyword for controlling inheritance and mutation in object-oriented design.', '2026-03-02 12:34:21'),
(75, 1, 2, 'Among the Components of DDBMS which one receives and processes the application requests?', 'In a Distributed Database Management System (DDBMS), the Transaction Processor (TP) or transaction manager receives and processes application requests, managing transaction execution across multiple sites. It handles coordination, concurrency control, and recovery. Data processors (DP) manage local data storage. DDBMS protocols define communication rules. Workstations are user interfaces. Understanding DDBMS architecture is important for designing scalable, fault-tolerant distributed systems, ensuring data consistency and availability across networks.', '2026-03-02 12:35:22'),
(76, 1, 2, 'What is the primary function of the Data Link Layer in the OSI model?\n\\', 'The Data Link Layer (Layer 2) is responsible for node-to-node data transfer over a physical link. It handles framing, physical addressing (MAC addresses), error detection/correction (CRC), flow control, and media access control (MAC). It ensures reliable communication between directly connected devices, unlike the Network Layer (routing) or Transport Layer (end-to-end reliability). Key protocols include Ethernet, Wi-Fi (MAC sublayer), PPP, and HDLC. Understanding this layer is essential for networking concepts like switches, VLANs, ARP, and data link technologies. It bridges the physical hardware and higher logical networking functions in the OSI and TCP/IP models.', '2026-03-02 12:36:33'),
(77, 1, 2, 'What is the purpose of a domain name system?', 'The primary purpose of DNS (Domain Name System) is to translate human-readable domain names (e.g., www.example.com) into machine-readable IP addresses (e.g., 192.0.2.1). It is a distributed hierarchical directory service. Getting a new domain involves registrars. Hosting documents is done by web servers. DNS is fundamental to internet functionality, enabling users to access resources by name rather than numeric addresses. Understanding DNS is crucial for network administration, web development, and cybersecurity (e.g., DNS poisoning attacks).', '2026-03-02 12:37:25'),
(78, 1, 2, 'A company wants to analyze their sales data to identify which products sell well together. Which SQL join would be used to accomplish this task?', 'An inner join returns rows where there is a match in both tables, ideal for finding related records like products sold together (market basket analysis). Outer joins (left, right, full) include unmatched rows. For association analysis, inner joins on transaction tables are used. Understanding join types is fundamental to SQL querying, enabling complex data retrieval from normalized databases. This skill is essential for data analysis, reporting, and application development involving relational data.', '2026-03-02 12:38:08'),
(79, 1, 2, 'What is the time complexity of Kruskal\'s algorithm for finding the minimum spanning tree?', 'Kruskal\'s algorithm uses a greedy approach: sort all edges by weight (O(E log E)) and iteratively add the smallest edge that doesn\'t form a cycle, using a Union-Find data structure for cycle detection (near O(1) per operation). Thus, overall complexity is dominated by sorting: O(E log E). Since E ≤ V^2, it\'s also O(E log V). Kruskal\'s is efficient for sparse graphs. Comparing with Prim\'s (O(E log V) with binary heap), choice depends on graph density and implementation. MST algorithms are crucial in network design, clustering, and approximation algorithms. Understanding their complexity helps in selecting the right algorithm for graph-based problems.', '2026-03-02 12:40:25'),
(80, 1, 2, 'In an E-R, Y is the dominant entity and X is a subordinate entity. Then which of the following is incorrect :', 'In an ER model, if Y is dominant and X is subordinate (existence-dependent), deletion of Y typically cascades to X (due to referential integrity constraints). However, deletion of X does not affect Y. The incorrect statement suggests deletion of X causes deletion of Y, which violates the dominance relationship. Understanding entity relationships and deletion rules is essential for database design, ensuring referential integrity and correct behavior in applications that modify related data.', '2026-03-02 12:41:16'),
(81, 1, 2, 'The Kleene star operation \"*\" in regular expressions represents:', 'The Kleene star (*) denotes zero or more repetitions of the preceding element. For example, a* matches \'\', \'a\', \'aa\', \'aaa\', ... The plus (+) denotes one or more repetitions. Concatenation is implicit sequencing. Union is the pipe (|). Regular expressions are used in lexical analysis, pattern matching, and text processing. Understanding RE operators is essential for writing patterns in tools like grep, sed, and programming language libraries, as well as for designing token specifiers in compilers.', '2026-03-02 12:42:09'),
(82, 1, 2, 'Analyze the following code snippet and identify the phase in which an undeclared variable error would be detected: int main() { int a = 5; b = a + 10; return 0;}', 'Undeclared variable errors are detected during semantic analysis, which checks context-sensitive rules like variable declaration before use. Lexical analysis identifies tokens (int, main, {, etc.). Syntax analysis checks grammar (structure). Code generation produces target code. Semantic analysis also includes type checking, scope resolution, and other meaning-related validations. Understanding compiler phases helps in debugging compilation errors and designing language processors that provide meaningful error messages to programmers.', '2026-03-02 12:42:56'),
(83, 1, 2, 'Which of the following search type is known by the use of heuristic search algorithm?', 'A* search is a heuristic search algorithm that uses a cost function f(n) = g(n) + h(n), where g(n) is the cost from the start to node n, and h(n) is a heuristic estimate of the cost from n to the goal. It\'s informed and often optimal if the heuristic is admissible. BFS and DFS are uninformed. Binary search is for sorted arrays. Heuristic search is crucial in AI for problems like pathfinding (maps, games), puzzle solving, and optimization where exhaustive search is impractical. Understanding heuristics and A* is key to efficient problem-solving in AI.', '2026-03-02 12:43:43'),
(84, 1, 2, 'Which algorithm is commonly used for classification tasks?', 'Logistic Regression is a fundamental classification algorithm for predicting categorical outcomes (binary or multinomial). Linear regression predicts continuous values. K-means is clustering (unsupervised). Association mining finds patterns (e.g., market basket analysis). Other common classifiers include Decision Trees, SVM, and Neural Networks. Understanding algorithm categories (regression, classification, clustering) is essential for selecting the right tool for a given machine learning problem, based on the nature of the target variable and the data available.', '2026-03-02 12:44:28'),
(85, 1, 2, 'Which one of the following is NOT the characteristics of CSS?', 'CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is for styling and layout of HTML documents. It separates content from presentation, leading to smaller HTML files, easier maintenance (less work), and precise control over typography and layout. However, CSS itself does not create dynamic content; that is the role of JavaScript or server-side scripting. Understanding the division of responsibilities in web development (HTML-structure, CSS-presentation, JavaScript-behavior) is fundamental to building modern, maintainable, and efficient web applications.', '2026-03-02 12:46:20'),
(86, 1, 2, 'Which attribute of input tag is used to group radio buttons?', 'The name attribute groups radio buttons together; only one radio button with the same name can be selected at a time within a form. The type must be \"radio\". The title provides a tooltip. There is no standard group attribute for input tags. Understanding HTML form elements and their attributes is crucial for creating interactive web pages that collect user input correctly and ensure data is properly associated for server-side processing or client-side scripting.', '2026-03-02 12:47:01'),
(87, 1, 2, 'Regular expression for all strings starts with 01 and ends with 110 is.', 'The regular expression must match strings that start with \'01\', have any sequence of 0s and 1s in the middle (including possibly empty), and end with \'110\'. 01(0+1)*110 correctly represents this: 01 literal, (0+1)* zero or more of 0 or 1, 110 literal. Option A limits the middle. Option B requires an odd-length middle repeating \'01\'. Option D is a specific string. Understanding regular expression construction is key for defining patterns in lexical analysis, string validation, and search operations in software development.', '2026-03-02 12:49:03'),
(88, 1, 2, 'If you set background image that is not large enough to cover the device\'s whole screen, which property allows you to repeat the picture to fill the space?', 'The CSS property background-repeat controls if/how a background image is repeated. The value repeat (default) tiles the image both horizontally and vertically to fill the element. Other values: no-repeat, repeat-x, repeat-y. background-image sets the image URL. background-position sets starting position. There is no background-fill. Understanding CSS background properties is essential for web design, enabling control over visual presentation and responsive layouts across different screen sizes and devices.', '2026-03-02 12:50:10'),
(89, 1, 2, 'The code segment below has time complexity? for (int i = 0; i < n; i++){ for (int j = 1; j < n: j=j ∗2){ int val = ( j ∗ i ) ; cout<< val; } }', 'The outer loop runs n times. The inner loop increments j by doubling it each time (j = j * 2), so it runs approximately log2(n) times (since j goes from 1 to n). Therefore, total operations ≈ n * log n, giving O(n log n) time complexity. This pattern is common in algorithms like certain divide-and-conquer operations or nested loops where the inner loop reduces problem size geometrically. Understanding how to analyze nested loops with non-linear updates is essential for accurate complexity analysis of algorithms.', '2026-03-02 12:53:36'),
(90, 1, 2, 'What is the key difference between a serial and a parallel interface?', 'The fundamental difference is transmission method: serial sends one bit per clock cycle over a single channel; parallel sends multiple bits simultaneously over multiple channels. Historically, parallel interfaces (e.g., Parallel ATA, IEEE 1284) were faster for short distances but suffered from skew and interference at high speeds. Modern serial interfaces (e.g., USB, PCI Express, SATA) use high clock rates and encoding to achieve higher speeds and better reliability over longer distances. Understanding interface types is important for computer hardware, peripheral connectivity, and system design.', '2026-03-02 12:54:59'),
(91, 1, 2, 'An agent that chooses its actions based on how satisfied or successful it will be in different situations, aiming for the most desirable outcome. Which type of agent is this?', 'A utility-based agent uses a utility function to measure the desirability of states. It chooses actions that maximize expected utility, handling trade-offs between multiple, possibly conflicting goals. A goal-based agent only knows goals. A simple reflex agent reacts to current percepts. A model-based agent maintains an internal state. Utility-based agents are more general and realistic in complex environments. Understanding agent architectures is fundamental to artificial intelligence, robotics, and autonomous systems design, where agents must make rational decisions under uncertainty.', '2026-03-02 12:59:25'),
(92, 1, 2, 'Which parsing technique uses a predictive approach and can handle recursive descent parsing without backtracking?', 'LL(1) parsing is a top-down, predictive parsing technique that uses one token lookahead to decide which production to apply, enabling recursive descent parsing without backtracking. It requires grammars to be LL(1), avoiding left recursion and ambiguity. LR parsing is bottom-up and more powerful. Understanding parsing techniques is key to compiler construction, language design, and syntactic analysis. LL parsers are often used in hand-written parsers and tools like ANTLR, while LR parsers are common in parser generators like Yacc/Bison.', '2026-03-02 13:01:21'),
(93, 1, 2, 'How many times will the following loop execute? int i = 0; while (i < 5) { cout << i << \" \"; i += 2; }', 'The loop starts with i=0. Each iteration prints i and increments i by 2. Iteration values: i=0 (print 0), i=2 (print 2), i=4 (print 4), i=6 (loop condition i<5 false). So, 3 iterations. Understanding loop execution, especially with non-unit increments, is fundamental to programming logic and algorithm design. This skill is essential for predicting program behavior, debugging, and writing correct loops for tasks like array traversal or numerical computation.', '2026-03-02 13:02:55'),
(94, 1, 2, 'Which complexity class represents decision problems that can be solved by a deterministic Turing Machine in polynomial time?', 'P (Polynomial time) is the class of decision problems solvable by a deterministic Turing machine in time polynomial in the input size. NP (Nondeterministic Polynomial time) are problems verifiable in polynomial time. Co-NP contains complements of NP problems. PSPACE are problems solvable with polynomial memory. The P vs NP question is a central open problem in computer science. Understanding complexity classes is essential for algorithm analysis, cryptography, and understanding which problems are tractable (efficiently solvable) versus intractable.', '2026-03-02 13:04:43'),
(95, 1, 2, 'What is the reason JavaScript is called loosely typed language?', 'JavaScript is loosely (dynamically) typed because variables are not bound to a specific data type; a variable can hold a number, then a string, then an object. Type conversions are performed implicitly by the engine. This contrasts with strongly typed languages like Java or C++. Loose typing offers flexibility but can lead to runtime type errors. Understanding type systems is important for programming language design, debugging, and writing robust code. Modern JavaScript (TypeScript) adds optional static typing to address some downsides of dynamic typing.', '2026-03-02 13:06:59'),
(96, 1, 1, 'Look at the following PHP line:\n$list = array(\"outside\" => \"red\", \"inside\" => \"brown\", \"top\" => \"black\", \"under\" => \"blue\");\nWhat is the index of data value black?', 'In this associative array, \'black\' has key \'top\'. PHP arrays can be indexed (numeric) or associative (string keys). Here, keys are strings: \'outside\', \'inside\', \'top\', \'under\' mapping to color values. Accessing $list[\'top\'] returns \'black\'. Associative arrays implement hash tables for efficient key-value lookups. They\'re fundamental for configuration, data transformation, and representing objects. PHP\'s array type serves multiple purposes: list, stack, queue, dictionary. Understanding array types and access methods is essential for PHP development. Modern PHP also supports short array syntax [\'key\' => \'value\'].', '2026-03-02 13:33:24'),
(97, 1, 2, 'How does the CPU\'s control unit coordinate the execution of instructions?', 'The control unit directs the operation of the processor. It fetches instructions from memory, decodes them to determine the operation, and then generates control signals to coordinate the ALU, registers, and other components to execute the instruction. This is the essence of the fetch-decode-execute cycle. Memory management is handled by the MMU. I/O operations are managed by I/O controllers. Understanding the control unit\'s role is central to computer architecture, explaining how software instructions are transformed into hardware actions.', '2026-03-02 13:36:05'),
(98, 1, 2, 'Which one of the following function is used to connect PHP with MySQL database', 'mysqli_connect() (or new mysqli()) is the function to establish a connection to a MySQL database in PHP\'s MySQLi extension. It takes host, username, password, and database name parameters. mysqli_select_db() selects a database after connection. mysql_query() is from the old, deprecated MySQL extension. Understanding database connectivity in PHP is fundamental for building data-driven web applications, enabling interaction with databases to store, retrieve, and manipulate information based on user requests.', '2026-03-02 13:37:44'),
(99, 1, 2, 'Consider a situation where a swap operation is very costly. Which of the following sorting algorithms should be preferred so that the number of swap operations is minimized in general?', 'Merge sort is a comparison-based algorithm that primarily uses copying of elements during the merge phase rather than in-place swaps. While it uses O(n) auxiliary space, it minimizes the number of swap operations compared to algorithms like Bubble Sort or Quick Sort that frequently swap elements in-place. Quick Sort has many swaps during partitioning. Bubble Sort is swap-intensive. Shell sort uses swaps with gaps. When swap cost dominates (e.g., sorting large records on disk), Merge sort\'s data movement pattern can be more efficient. Understanding algorithm costs (comparisons, swaps, space) is crucial for selecting the right sort for a given context.', '2026-03-02 13:41:57'),
(100, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is false about abstract classes and abstract methods?', 'Abstract classes CANNOT be instantiated directly—they require concrete subclasses. The \'new\' operator creates objects of concrete classes. Abstract methods declare signatures without implementation, forcing subclasses to provide concrete implementations. Classes containing abstract methods must be declared abstract. Abstract classes can contain both abstract and concrete methods, enabling partial implementation. They define interfaces for related classes, supporting polymorphism and template method patterns. In languages like Java and C#, abstract classes differ from interfaces (which contain only method declarations). Understanding abstraction is key to framework design and code organization.', '2026-03-02 13:43:09'),
(101, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is not correct about IPv4?', 'IPv4 uses 32-bit addresses (approximately 4.3 billion unique addresses), not 128-bit. IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses. IPv4\'s limited address space led to NAT (Network Address Translation) and CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing). While IPv4 routing is efficient in practice, larger routing tables challenge memory. The address exhaustion prompted IPv6 adoption. IPv4 addresses are dotted-decimal (e.g., 192.168.1.1), while IPv6 uses hexadecimal groups (e.g., 2001:0db8::1). Transition mechanisms like dual stacking facilitate migration. Understanding addressing limitations explains Internet growth challenges and protocol evolution.', '2026-03-02 13:44:28'),
(102, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is false about client-side scripting?', 'Client-side scripts (JavaScript, VBScript) typically CANNOT directly access client hardware due to security sandboxing. They run in browsers with restricted access to file system, devices, and OS. Scripts are indeed embedded as plain text, interpreted (or JIT-compiled), and have fewer features than system programming languages. Security restrictions prevent malicious code from harming user systems. Exceptions exist with privileged contexts (browser extensions, signed scripts) or new APIs (WebUSB, File System Access) with user permission. Understanding script limitations explains web security model and the rise of hybrid apps (Electron, NW.js) bridging web-native capabilities.', '2026-03-02 13:45:49'),
(103, 1, 1, 'Given input Alphabet = {c, d} and a language (L) defined on Σ as L = {c^n d^n : n > 0}. Then, which one of the following is not an element of language L?', 'The language L contains strings with equal numbers of \'c\'s followed by equal numbers of \'d\'s, with n > 0. \'Ccddd\' has 2 c\'s and 3 d\'s (unequal), so it\'s not in L. \'Cd\' has 1 c and 1 d (n=1), \'ccccdddd\' has 4 c\'s and 4 d\'s (n=4), and \'ccdd\' has 2 c\'s and 2 d\'s (n=2). This language is context-free but not regular, famously recognized by pushdown automata but not finite automata. It\'s a classic example in automata theory demonstrating limitations of regular expressions.', '2026-03-02 13:47:50'),
(105, 1, 1, 'When we use a hash table data structure, our hash function should map the expected inputs (keys) as evenly as possible over its output range (indexes). This property of good hash function is known as', 'Uniformity in hash functions means keys are distributed evenly across the hash table\'s buckets, minimizing collisions and ensuring O(1) average access time. Determinism (same key always produces same hash) and efficiency are also important properties. Continuity is undesirable in hash functions as similar keys should produce very different hashes. Data normalization refers to database design principles. A uniform distribution prevents clustering and maintains hash table performance regardless of key distribution patterns.', '2026-03-02 13:50:05'),
(106, 1, 1, 'In an array implementation of binary heap tree, if a node is stored as index i, then its right child will be stored as index ______.', 'In array-based binary heap implementations (0-indexed or 1-indexed), children of node at index i are typically at indices 2i and 2i+1 for 1-based indexing, or 2i+1 and 2i+2 for 0-based indexing. The question likely assumes 0-based indexing where left child = 2i+1 and right child = 2i+2, but given the options, 2*i+1 would be right child in 1-based indexing if left is 2*i. For clarity: in 0-based arrays, left=2i+1, right=2i+2; parent=floor((i-1)/2). This compact representation enables efficient heap operations.', '2026-03-02 13:51:08'),
(107, 1, 2, 'In SQL the statement select * from R, S is equivalent to', 'In SQL, SELECT * FROM R, S performs a Cartesian product (cross join) of tables R and S, returning all row combinations. This is equivalent to CROSS JOIN. An inner join requires a condition. Natural join matches same-named columns. Union join is not standard. Understanding join syntax is essential for writing correct SQL queries and avoiding unintended Cartesian products that can produce massive result sets and performance issues.', '2026-03-02 13:51:13'),
(108, 1, 1, 'Which of the following is not the function of DHCP server?', 'Option D is poorly worded but essentially duplicates A with \'sender\' instead of \'client\'. However, DHCP responds to client requests, not \'sender\' requests—terminology matters. Functions include: address allocation, configuration delivery (subnet mask, gateway, DNS), lease management, address database maintenance. DHCP uses UDP ports 67 (server) and 68 (client). Understanding DHCP roles explains network auto-configuration. Advanced features: reservations, scopes, relay agents, DHCPv6 for IPv6. Misconfigurations cause network issues. The protocol\'s broadcast nature requires relays across subnets. Distinguishing server vs. client roles clarifies network service architecture.', '2026-03-02 13:52:49'),
(109, 1, 1, '______ is a way of combining analogue or digital signals into one single over a common or shared medium.', 'Multicasting involves transmitting data to a group of destinations simultaneously over a shared medium, efficiently using bandwidth for one-to-many communications. Point-to-point connects two endpoints directly, broadcasting sends to all nodes on a network, and synchronization coordinates timing between systems. While multiplexing (combining multiple signals) might be a better answer, multicasting represents a specific method of shared medium communication used in IP networks for applications like video conferencing, online gaming, and content distribution.', '2026-03-02 13:53:48'),
(110, 1, 1, '______ Uses the knowledge of the characteristics of the relational algebra operations and the relationship between the operators to optimize the query?', 'Heuristic query optimization uses rule-based strategies (heuristics) to transform queries into more efficient forms, such as \'perform selections early\' or \'combine Cartesian products with subsequent selections into joins\'. Unlike cost-based optimization that estimates actual execution costs, heuristic optimization applies proven rules that generally improve performance. These heuristics leverage knowledge of relational algebra properties like commutativity of selections and joins. While less precise than cost-based methods, heuristic optimization requires less statistical information and works well for common query patterns.', '2026-03-02 13:56:57'),
(111, 1, 1, 'What should compilers of high level programming languages do to evaluate expressions?', 'Compilers typically convert infix expressions (operators between operands: a+b) to postfix (Reverse Polish: ab+) for evaluation using stack machines. Postfix eliminates parentheses and operator precedence issues, enabling straightforward stack-based evaluation. Some compilers generate abstract syntax trees directly. Prefix notation (Polish: +ab) is also parenthesis-free but less common. The conversion uses the Shunting Yard algorithm (Dijkstra) respecting precedence and associativity. Understanding this process illuminates how expressions become machine instructions. Modern compilers optimize expression evaluation through constant folding, common subexpression elimination, and instruction selection.', '2026-03-02 13:58:54'),
(112, 1, 1, 'What is not a Functional Requirement?', 'Option D is NOT a functional requirement—it\'s a statement about what requirements don\'t specify. Functional requirements define system behavior (positive and negative). Non-functional requirements describe quality attributes (performance, security, usability). Clear requirements avoid ambiguity. Examples: \'system shall process 1000 transactions/second\' (non-functional), \'system shall calculate interest\' (functional). Requirements engineering classifies and prioritizes needs. Misclassification leads to incorrect testing and validation. Understanding the distinction ensures complete specification covering both what the system does and how well it does it. This foundational concept guides software development from inception to delivery.', '2026-03-02 14:07:30'),
(113, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is not true?', 'Kernel modules CAN be loaded/unloaded at runtime (loadable kernel modules - LKMs). This allows extending kernel functionality without rebooting. The kernel is indeed the core OS component, loaded first by bootloader, resident in memory. Modules enable drivers and features to be added dynamically. Examples: Linux kernel modules (.ko files). Understanding kernel architecture distinguishes monolithic (all in one) vs. microkernel (minimal) designs. Modern OSes use hybrid approaches. Kernel mode vs. user mode provides protection. This flexibility supports hardware hot-plugging and reduces memory footprint by loading only needed components.', '2026-03-02 14:08:50'),
(114, 1, 1, 'What is the output of the following C++ fragment code?\nint p, q, n;\np = q = n = 8;\np += n;\np /= q;\ncout << p;', 'The code executes: p = q = n = 8 (all variables become 8). p += n means p = p + n = 8 + 8 = 16. p /= q means p = p / q = 16 / 8 = 2. Integer division yields 2 (not 2.0, as integers truncate decimals). The output is 2. This demonstrates compound assignment operators (+=, /=) and chained assignment. Understanding operator precedence and integer arithmetic is crucial for correct predictions. Note that if variables were floats, division would produce 2.0, but output formatting might show differences. In C++, integer division truncates toward zero.', '2026-03-02 14:10:47'),
(115, 1, 3, 'Which one of the following is not a component of the computer system?', 'Standard computer components include: input unit (keyboard, mouse), output unit (display, printer), storage unit (memory, disk), arithmetic logic unit (ALU - calculations), control unit (CU - instruction decoding, timing). The \'data unit\' isn\'t a recognized component. The CPU combines ALU and CU. Memory hierarchy: registers, cache, RAM, secondary storage. I/O devices handle data transfer. Bus system connects components (address, data, control buses). Modern systems add GPU, network interfaces, and specialized processors. All components work together under stored-program concept where instructions and data share memory.', '2026-03-02 14:11:15'),
(116, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is a communication pathway connecting transmitting signals in binary digits between two or more devices?', 'A bus is a communication system that transfers data between computer components using shared signal paths. It carries binary data (bits), addresses, and control signals. Types include data bus (actual data), address bus (memory locations), and control bus (commands). Memory stores data, registers are small storage within CPU, CPU processes data. Buses can be parallel (multiple bits simultaneously) or serial (one bit at a time). Modern computers use hierarchical bus architectures (front-side bus, memory bus, I/O buses) with bridges connecting different speed domains. Bus width and clock rate determine bandwidth.', '2026-03-02 14:12:02'),
(117, 1, 3, 'If a problem can be broken into sub-problems that are reused several times, the problem possesses property.', 'Overlapping sub-problems is a key property in dynamic programming where the same sub-problems are solved multiple times during computation. This property allows optimization through techniques like memoization (caching results) to avoid redundant calculations. Optimal substructure refers to problems where optimal solutions can be constructed from optimal solutions of sub-problems. Greedy algorithms make locally optimal choices. The presence of overlapping sub-problems makes dynamic programming more efficient than naive recursive solutions.', '2026-03-02 14:12:42'),
(118, 1, 1, 'Which symbol is used to write a single line comment in JavaScript?', 'In JavaScript, single-line comments begin with \'//\' and extend to the end of the line. This is standard across most C-style languages including Java, C++, and C#. \'<!-- -->\' is HTML/XML comment syntax, \'<?\' is used in PHP and some templating languages, and \'&&\' is the logical AND operator in JavaScript. Multi-line comments in JavaScript use /* ... */ syntax. Understanding comment syntax is fundamental for code documentation and temporary code exclusion during debugging.', '2026-03-02 14:13:29'),
(119, 1, 1, 'Which method can be used to divide a browser into smaller sub windows so that each sub window displaying a different HTML document?', 'HTML frames (using <frameset> and <frame> tags) allow dividing browser windows into multiple independent sections, each loading separate HTML documents. Though deprecated in HTML5 in favor of <iframe> and CSS layouts, frames were historically used for navigation menus, headers, and sidebars. Paragraphs (<p>) format text, images (<img>) display graphics, and tables (<table>) organize data. Modern alternatives include CSS grid/flexbox for layout and <iframe> for embedded documents, but frames provided the original method for partitioned browser windows.', '2026-03-02 14:14:43'),
(120, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is not correct when mapping ER diagrams to relational tables?', 'Composite attributes in ER diagrams should be decomposed into their simple components when mapping to relational tables, not mapped \'as is\'. For example, a \'Name\' composite attribute consisting of \'FirstName\', \'MiddleName\', and \'LastName\' should be stored as separate columns. Multi-valued attributes do require separate tables, foreign keys correctly implement relationships, and table names typically correspond to entity names. Proper decomposition of composite attributes ensures first normal form (1NF) compliance and facilitates efficient querying and indexing in the relational model.', '2026-03-02 14:16:21'),
(121, 1, 1, '________________ is responsible for converting the higher level protocol addresses to physical Network Addresses.', 'ARP maps IP addresses to MAC addresses on local networks. It broadcasts \'who has IP X?\' and the owner replies with its MAC. RARP does reverse (MAC to IP), BOOTP assigns IP addresses, ICMP handles error messages (ping). ARP is essential for Ethernet/IP networks, maintaining cache tables. ARP spoofing attacks exploit this trust model. Understanding ARP explains how layer 3 (IP) interfaces with layer 2 (Ethernet). Without ARP, IP packets couldn\'t be encapsulated in frames for delivery. Related protocols: Proxy ARP, Gratuitous ARP. This address resolution is a fundamental networking concept.', '2026-03-02 14:17:58'),
(122, 1, 1, 'What is not a Functional Requirement?', 'Option D is NOT a functional requirement—it\'s a statement about what requirements don\'t specify, not a requirement itself. Functional requirements define system behaviors: what it must do (positive) and must not do (negative/constraints). Non-functional requirements describe quality attributes: performance, security, usability, etc. Clear requirements avoid ambiguity and enable testing. Misclassification leads to incorrect validation. Requirements engineering involves classification, prioritization, and verification. Understanding this distinction ensures comprehensive specification covering both capabilities (functional) and qualities (non-functional). This foundational concept guides software development from inception through delivery and maintenance.', '2026-03-02 14:19:23'),
(123, 1, 1, 'Which theory provides mechanisms for classifying combinatorial problems and measuring the computational resources necessary to solve them?', 'Computational complexity theory classifies problems by their inherent difficulty and the resources (time, space) required to solve them. It establishes classes like P (polynomial time), NP (nondeterministic polynomial), and NP-complete. Computability theory (recursion theory) determines what problems can be solved algorithmically. Automata theory studies abstract machines and languages. Set theory is mathematical foundation. Complexity theory\'s classifications guide algorithm design and help identify when approximate or heuristic solutions are necessary for intractable problems.', '2026-03-02 14:20:51'),
(124, 1, 1, 'Which memory is characterized by the need for periodic change in order to maintain data storage?', 'Dynamic RAM (DRAM) requires periodic refresh cycles to maintain data because it stores bits as charges in capacitors that leak. Refresh typically every 64ms. Static RAM (SRAM) uses flip-flops needing no refresh but is more expensive. Disks and tapes are non-volatile storage. DRAM\'s simplicity allows high density, making it main memory in computers. The refresh overhead (1-2% of bandwidth) is managed by memory controllers. DRAM technology has evolved (SDRAM, DDR) but retains refresh requirements. Understanding this distinction explains why computers need power for memory retention and why SRAM caches exist alongside DRAM.', '2026-03-02 14:22:23'),
(125, 1, 1, 'A malicious program that once installed on your machine can collect all keystrokes including username, password, emails, credit card numbers and other sensitive information is:', 'Keyloggers specifically capture keystrokes, often for credential theft. They can be hardware devices or software, sometimes bundled with Trojans. Rootkits hide malware presence, worms self-propagate, Trojans disguise as legitimate software. Keyloggers operate at kernel or user level, intercepting keyboard interrupts or API calls. Defenses include antivirus software, virtual keyboards, and behavioral detection. Some keyloggers also capture screenshots, clipboard contents, and network traffic. They\'re used in cyber espionage, identity theft, and surveillance. Legitimate uses exist for parental control or employee monitoring where legally permitted.', '2026-03-02 14:23:40'),
(126, 1, 1, '______ is the component of the operating system that is responsible to decide which process to run first among ready processes.', 'The scheduler (or process scheduler) selects which ready process gains CPU access, implementing scheduling policies (FCFS, Round Robin, Priority, etc.). Memory manager handles allocation, IPC enables process communication, disk manager controls storage. Scheduling algorithms balance criteria like throughput, latency, fairness, and responsiveness. Modern systems use multi-level feedback queues combining different policies. The scheduler is core to OS multitasking, context switching between processes to create the illusion of simultaneous execution while optimizing resource utilization and meeting performance objectives.', '2026-03-02 14:26:22'),
(127, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is not a condition for two operations in a schedule to be said to conflict?', 'Conflicting operations must belong to DIFFERENT transactions. Operations from the same transaction are ordered by transaction logic and don\'t conflict. Additional conditions: access same data item X, and at least one is Write_item(X). Read-Read pairs don\'t conflict. Conflicts cause serializability issues requiring concurrency control. Conflict serializability is tested via precedence graphs. Understanding conflicts guides locking protocols, timestamp ordering, and optimistic concurrency control. Database systems detect conflicts to maintain isolation while maximizing concurrency. This concept is fundamental to transaction processing in databases and distributed systems.', '2026-03-02 14:28:06'),
(128, 1, 3, 'Which of the following is Uninformed (Blind) Search?', 'Breadth-first search is an uninformed/blind search algorithm that explores all nodes at the present depth before moving deeper, using no domain-specific knowledge. It guarantees optimal solutions (if costs are equal) but has exponential space complexity. Other uninformed searches include depth-first search, uniform-cost search, and iterative deepening. Informed/heuristic searches (hill climbing, greedy search, A*) use problem-specific knowledge to guide search more efficiently. BFS is complete (finds solution if exists) but may be inefficient for large spaces. It\'s used in shortest path finding, web crawling, and network broadcasting.', '2026-03-02 14:28:08'),
(129, 1, 3, 'In PHP, are predefined variables that are accessible throughout your script.', 'PHP superglobals are automatically available in all scopes: $_GET (URL parameters), $_POST (form data), $_REQUEST (GET+POST+COOKIE), $_SESSION (session data), $_COOKIE (cookies), $_SERVER (server/environment info), $_ENV (environment variables), $_FILES (file uploads). They\'re associative arrays. Unlike regular globals (declared with global keyword), superglobals don\'t need global declaration. Security: sanitize superglobal inputs to prevent injection attacks. $_GET vs $_POST: GET for idempotent requests (bookmarkable), POST for data modification. Modern PHP practices recommend filtering input (filter_input) and avoiding $_REQUEST.', '2026-03-02 14:29:14'),
(130, 1, 1, '______ is a software development life cycle model that is chosen if the development team has less experience on similar projects.', 'The Waterfall model\'s linear, sequential approach is often recommended for inexperienced teams because it provides clear structure with defined phases: requirements, design, implementation, testing, maintenance. Each phase must be completed before the next begins, minimizing uncertainty and providing clear milestones. Iterative, RAD, and Spiral models require more experience to handle evolving requirements, rapid prototyping, and risk management. Waterfall\'s documentation emphasis helps knowledge transfer but can create rigidity. Team experience significantly influences methodology choice, alongside factors like requirements stability, project complexity, and risk tolerance. Sometimes structured hybrids provide balance for growing teams.', '2026-03-02 14:29:29'),
(131, 1, 1, 'First design the very high level structure of the system. Then gradually work down to detailed decisions about low-level constructs. Which design approach follows the given strategy?', 'Top-down design starts with high-level abstractions and progressively refines them into detailed implementations. This approach promotes modularity, simplifies understanding of complex systems, and facilitates parallel development. Bottom-up begins with components and assembles them. Hybrid combines both. System design encompasses multiple approaches. Top-down is particularly effective when requirements are clear at the system level but details evolve. It aligns with stepwise refinement methodology and hierarchical decomposition, common in structured programming and software architecture.', '2026-03-02 14:31:47'),
(132, 1, 1, 'Which of the following is the understanding of software product limitations, learning system related problems or changes to be done in existing systems beforehand, identifying and addressing the impact of project on organization and personnel etc.?', 'Feasibility Study assesses whether a project should be undertaken by evaluating technical, economic, legal, operational, and scheduling feasibility. It identifies constraints, risks, and organizational impacts before commitment. Requirements gathering collects needs, System Analysis studies existing systems, Software Design creates solutions. The feasibility study answers: Can we build it? Should we build it? It prevents wasted effort on impractical projects. Components: technical feasibility (technology available?), economic (cost-benefit), legal (compliance), operational (fits organization?), schedule (timeframe realistic?). This early-phase analysis guides go/no-go decisions and shapes project scope and approach.', '2026-03-02 14:33:07'),
(133, 1, 1, 'What will be the output of the following JavaScript statement? Math.abs(-9)', 'Math.abs() returns the absolute value (non-negative magnitude) of a number. For -9, absolute value is 9. This mathematical function is useful for distance calculations, error margins, and ensuring positive values. JavaScript\'s Math object provides mathematical functions and constants. Other useful methods include Math.floor(), Math.ceil(), Math.round(), Math.max(), Math.min(), and Math.random(). Understanding these built-in functions improves code efficiency and readability. Math.abs() handles edge cases: Math.abs(null) returns 0, Math.abs(\'string\') returns NaN. It\'s implemented across programming languages with similar semantics.', '2026-03-02 14:34:15'),
(134, 1, 3, 'What is a foreign key in a relational database?', 'A foreign key is a column or set of columns in a relational database table that establishes a link between data in two tables. It references the primary key of another table, creating referential integrity constraints that maintain consistent relationships. Foreign keys enforce relationships like one-to-many (customer to orders) and prevent orphaned records. They enable JOIN operations for data retrieval across tables. Unlike primary keys (unique identifiers), foreign keys allow duplicates and nulls (unless constrained). Proper foreign key design is essential for normalized database schemas and data integrity.', '2026-03-02 14:34:58'),
(135, 1, 1, 'What is the output of the following java fragment code?\nFor (int i = 2; i <= 11; I = I + 2)\n{', 'Loop values: i = 2,4,6,8,10 (increments by 2, stops at 11). Condition i%4==0 is true for 4 and 8. When i=4: prints 4*4=16. When i=8: prints 8*8=64. But wait—the answer shows 4 16 36 64 100, suggesting perhaps the code prints i*i for ALL i, not just when i%4==0? Actually, looking closely: there\'s a syntax error—parentheses mismatch. If corrected to print i*i for all i: 2²=4, 4²=16, 6²=36, 8²=64, 10²=100. So output: 4 16 36 64 100. This tests loop execution and conditional logic understanding.', '2026-03-02 14:35:05'),
(136, 1, 3, 'What is the primary objective of reinforcement learning in artificial intelligence?', 'Reinforcement learning (RL) focuses on training agents to make optimal decisions by interacting with an environment to maximize cumulative reward over time. Unlike supervised learning (classification/regression) or unsupervised learning (clustering), RL deals with sequential decision-making problems where actions affect future states and rewards. Agents learn through trial and error using concepts like exploration vs exploitation, value functions, and policy optimization. Applications include game playing, robotics, recommendation systems, and autonomous systems where delayed rewards and long-term consequences matter.', '2026-03-02 14:36:04'),
(137, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is false regarding Exceptions in Java?', 'In Java, developers CAN create custom exception classes by extending Exception (checked exceptions) or RuntimeException (unchecked exceptions). The Exception class handles recoverable conditions, RuntimeException handles programming errors, and all exceptions inherit from Throwable. Custom exceptions improve code clarity by representing domain-specific error conditions. They should provide constructors matching superclass patterns and often add relevant context information. Creating exception hierarchies helps organize error handling and enables catch blocks to handle related exceptions appropriately.', '2026-03-02 14:36:24'),
(138, 1, 1, '______ is a software development life cycle model that is chosen if the development team has less experience on similar projects.\n.', 'The Waterfall model\'s structured, sequential approach provides clear guidance for inexperienced teams with its phase-gate process: complete requirements before design, design before implementation, etc. This reduces uncertainty and provides defined milestones. Iterative, RAD, and Spiral models require more experience to handle evolving requirements, rapid prototyping, and risk management. Waterfall\'s emphasis on documentation aids knowledge transfer but can create rigidity. Team experience significantly influences methodology choice, alongside project factors like requirements stability, complexity, and risk. Sometimes hybrid approaches (structured phases with controlled iterations) balance control and flexibility for teams developing expertise.', '2026-03-02 14:37:25'),
(139, 1, 3, 'Which function of a contemporary operating system focuses on providing users with easy access to applications and resources?', 'UI management provides interaction mechanisms: CLI (command line), GUI (graphical), touch, voice. Components: window managers, desktop environments, shell programs. Good UI design principles: consistency, feedback, error prevention. Accessibility features aid diverse users. Modern OSs offer multiple UI options and customization. Underlying services: input device handling, display drivers, rendering. UI sits atop other OS functions. Trends: natural language interfaces, augmented reality, adaptive interfaces. UI significantly affects user productivity and satisfaction.', '2026-03-02 14:37:31'),
(140, 1, 1, 'In which step of SDLC actual programming of software code is done?', 'The Development phase is where coding transforms design specifications into executable software. This includes programming, unit testing, integration, and accompanying documentation. Analysis determines what to build, Design creates the blueprint, Maintenance occurs post-deployment. In Agile methodologies, these phases may overlap in iterations, but coding remains central to development. Effective development requires programming skills, tools (IDEs, version control), testing frameworks, and team collaboration. Understanding phase sequencing prevents premature coding (before requirements clarity) or implementation delays. This phase typically consumes significant resources and requires careful management of code quality, schedules, and team coordination to produce working software.', '2026-03-02 14:38:57'),
(141, 1, 3, 'Polynomial addition is implemented using which data structure?', 'Linked lists efficiently represent polynomials: each node contains coefficient and exponent. Addition merges two sorted lists (by exponent), combining coefficients for matching exponents. Advantages: dynamic size, easy insertion/deletion. Arrays can also store polynomials but waste space for sparse polynomials. Operations: addition (O(m+n)), multiplication (O(m*n)), evaluation (Horner\'s method). Polynomial representation extends to multi-variable using nested lists or trees. Applications: symbolic mathematics, computer algebra systems, signal processing, interpolation. Understanding polynomial operations demonstrates data structure selection based on operation patterns.', '2026-03-02 14:39:50'),
(142, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following best describes for a program that seems friendly and legitimate but contains hidden malicious codes and harms a system?', 'A Trojan horse appears useful or harmless but contains malicious code. Named after the Greek myth, Trojans rely on social engineering—users willingly install them. Unlike viruses (self-replicating by attaching to files) or worms (self-propagating across networks), Trojans don\'t replicate themselves. Malware is the general category including all malicious software. Trojans often create backdoors, steal data, or download additional malware. Examples include fake antivirus software, games with hidden payloads, or pirated software bundles. Defense involves cautious downloading, verifying signatures, and using security software that detects suspicious behavior.', '2026-03-02 14:40:13'),
(143, 1, 3, 'Which one is correct about RISC computers?', 'RISC (Reduced Instruction Set Computer) architecture features: simple, fixed-length instructions that execute in one cycle; hardwired control units for faster instruction decoding; extensive pipelining for parallel instruction execution; and larger register sets to reduce memory accesses. This contrasts with CISC (Complex Instruction Set Computer) which has variable-length instructions, microcoded control units, complex addressing modes, and instructions that may take multiple cycles. RISC designs enable higher clock speeds and better compiler optimization, while CISC offers denser code. Modern processors often use hybrid approaches.', '2026-03-02 14:41:13'),
(144, 1, 1, 'Which SQL command would you use if you want to specify constraints to be applied on tuples?', 'The CHECK constraint in SQL specifies conditions that each row must satisfy, enforcing domain integrity at the tuple level. It can be defined during table creation (CREATE TABLE) or added later (ALTER TABLE). GRANT manages privileges, SELECT retrieves data, REVOKE removes privileges. CHECK constraints validate data against business rules, e.g., ensuring age > 0 or salary within range. Unlike triggers which execute code, CHECK constraints are declarative and optimized by the DBMS. They prevent invalid data insertion/updates, maintaining data quality. However, complex business logic may require triggers or application-level validation.', '2026-03-02 14:41:32'),
(145, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is concerned with the meaning of a sentence in knowledge representation?', 'Semantics deals with the meaning and interpretation of sentences in knowledge representation. It focuses on what information is being conveyed, rather than just the structure (syntax) or implementation details (computational aspects). In natural language processing and knowledge representation systems, semantics ensures that the system understands the intended meaning behind expressions, which is crucial for tasks like reasoning, inference, and accurate information retrieval.', '2026-03-02 14:42:27'),
(146, 1, 3, 'What would be the output of the following JavaScript code? x=4+\'4\'; document.write(x);', 'JavaScript performs type coercion: when adding number and string, the number converts to string, resulting in concatenation \'44\' (not addition 8). The + operator is overloaded: numeric addition vs string concatenation. Rules: if either operand is string, concatenation occurs. Other arithmetic operators (-, *, /) convert to numbers. Type coercion can cause subtle bugs. Best practice: explicit conversion using Number(), String(), or parseInt(). Modern JavaScript offers template literals for cleaner string construction. Understanding operator behavior is crucial for avoiding unexpected results in dynamic typing.', '2026-03-02 14:43:48'),
(147, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is not correct about networking models?', 'OSI is a reference model (conceptual framework), not a protocol model with implementation specifications. TCP/IP is both a reference model and an actual protocol suite. Reference models standardize concepts; protocol models define actual implementations. Network designers often use hybrid approaches combining concepts from both models. The OSI model\'s seven layers (physical, data link, network, transport, session, presentation, application) provide a teaching tool, while TCP/IP\'s four layers (link, internet, transport, application) reflect Internet architecture. Understanding this distinction clarifies why OSI isn\'t directly implemented while TCP/IP powers the Internet.', '2026-03-02 14:43:52'),
(148, 1, 1, 'Which network address translation protocol solves the task of address resolution using port number?', 'Port Address Translation (PAT), also called NAT overload, enables multiple devices to share a single public IP address by using different port numbers to distinguish connections. Standard NAT provides one-to-one mapping, while PAT creates many-to-one mappings. This conserves IPv4 addresses and is commonly used in home routers. PAT tracks connections via source port rewriting in packet headers. Dynamic NAT assigns addresses from a pool, Static NAT uses fixed mappings. Understanding NAT types explains network configurations: PAT for client access, static NAT for servers, dynamic NAT for temporary needs. NAT traversal remains challenging for P2P applications, requiring techniques like STUN and ICE.', '2026-03-02 14:44:46'),
(149, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following sorting algorithm requires large memory space to sort data?', 'Merge sort requires O(n) additional memory space for its merge operations, making it memory-intensive compared to in-place sorts. Insertion and selection sorts use O(1) extra space, quicksort uses O(log n) stack space on average. Merge sort\'s stability and guaranteed O(n log n) performance come at the cost of space. External merge sort handles datasets larger than memory using disk. In memory-constrained environments, in-place algorithms like heapsort (O(1) space) are preferable. The space-time tradeoff illustrates algorithm design principles: merge sort sacrifices space for predictable performance and stability.', '2026-03-02 14:45:45'),
(150, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is false about the role of parser in compiling process?', 'Parsers DO perform context-free syntax analysis, checking if token sequences conform to the language grammar. They construct parse trees (concrete or abstract), report syntax errors, and often implement error recovery strategies (panic mode, phrase-level recovery). Parsers can be top-down (LL) or bottom-up (LR), with different capabilities. While lexical analysis uses regular expressions, parsing requires context-free grammars for nested structures like parentheses and statements. Modern parsers often generate abstract syntax trees (ASTs) for semantic analysis. Error recovery helps continue parsing after errors for multiple error reporting.', '2026-03-02 14:47:37'),
(151, 1, 1, 'Look at the following fragment code:\nstruct car {\nchar car_model[25];\nfloat price;\n};', 'c1 is a pointer to car structure. To access members: c1->price or (*c1).price. Option C correctly uses c1.price assuming c1 is not a pointer? Wait, c1 IS declared as pointer (car *c1). So c1.price would be incorrect—should be c1->price. But among options, C is closest if we consider c1 might be dereferenced implicitly? Actually, looking carefully: option C writes c1.price = 150000; which would be wrong syntax for pointer. Possibly the intended correct answer assumes c1 is an object, not pointer. Given typical usage: for pointer p, p->member; for object o, o.member. This tests structure member access syntax.', '2026-03-02 14:48:43'),
(152, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following circuits requires memory to store the state of the output?', 'Sequential circuits have memory elements (flip-flops, latches) that store state, making output dependent on both current inputs AND past states. Combinational circuits produce outputs based solely on current inputs (no memory). \'Logical circuit\' is ambiguous, \'physical circuit\' refers to implementation. Sequential circuits enable counters, registers, finite state machines, and memory units. Clock signals synchronize state changes. Understanding this distinction is fundamental to digital design: combinational circuits handle arithmetic/logic operations, sequential circuits manage control and state. Most digital systems combine both—datapaths (combinational) with control units (sequential)—enabling complex behaviors beyond immediate input responses.', '2026-03-02 14:50:38'),
(153, 1, 1, 'Software Debugging is known as ________________', 'Debugging specifically refers to locating and fixing defects in code after they\'re discovered (usually through testing). It\'s a critical skill involving: problem diagnosis, root cause analysis, solution implementation, and verification. Tools: debuggers, profilers, log analyzers. Techniques: breakpoints, stepping, watch expressions, memory inspection. Effective debugging requires systematic approaches rather than random changes. Related but distinct activities: requirements analysis (task identification), coding (implementation), algorithm design (solution strategy). Debugging often consumes significant development time; preventive measures (code reviews, unit testing, static analysis) reduce this burden. Mastering debugging improves software quality and developer productivity.', '2026-03-02 14:52:21'),
(154, 1, 1, 'Given the network address of 172.16.0.0/19, which of the following is correct?', '/19 means 19 network bits, 13 host bits (32-19). Hosts per subnet: 2^13 - 2 = 8190 (subtract network and broadcast). Subnets: depends on how many bits borrowed beyond classful? Actually /19 is CIDR notation, not indicating subnet count directly. But if we consider class B default /16, borrowing 3 bits gives 8 subnets (2^3). So: 8 subnets, each with 8190 usable hosts. Understanding subnetting is crucial for network design: balancing subnet count vs. host capacity. CIDR notation compactly represents mask: /19 = 255.255.224.0. This skill is essential for IP planning and allocation.', '2026-03-02 14:53:23'),
(155, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is an attack performed by wiretapping a network and illicitly copying files and programs?', 'Interception involves unauthorized access to information during transmission, typically through methods like wiretapping, eavesdropping, or packet sniffing. Unlike other attacks, interception doesn\'t alter or create data but focuses on secretly capturing it. Fabrication involves creating false data, interruption denies service, and modification alters existing data. Interception is particularly dangerous because it can be passive and undetectable while gathering sensitive information like passwords, files, or confidential communications.', '2026-03-02 14:55:57'),
(156, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is not transmission impairments?', 'Synchronization is a technique to coordinate transmitter and receiver timing, not an impairment. Transmission impairments degrade signal quality: attenuation (loss of strength), distortion (change in shape), noise (unwanted signals). Crosstalk is interference between adjacent channels, impulse noise is sudden high-energy disturbances. Synchronization problems cause errors but aren\'t impairments per se—they\'re protocol or timing issues. Impairments are physical layer phenomena affecting analog/digital signals during propagation. Understanding impairments guides communication system design: equalization compensates distortion, shielding reduces crosstalk, repeaters counteract attenuation.', '2026-03-02 14:58:27'),
(157, 1, 1, 'Which data structure is the most appropriate for sharing a printer in a network environment?\n\nA) Stack', 'Queues implement First-In-First-Out (FIFO) ordering, perfect for printer sharing where jobs should be processed in arrival order. Stacks (LIFO) would unfairly prioritize recent jobs. Trees organize hierarchical data, not sequential access. Print spoolers use queues to manage fairness and prevent conflicts. Network print servers maintain job queues with priorities, scheduling, and error handling. Queue operations (enqueue, dequeue) efficiently manage job flow. Additional features like job cancellation, status monitoring, and priority queues enhance functionality. Understanding appropriate data structures for real-world problems is key to effective system design.', '2026-03-02 14:59:50'),
(158, 1, 3, 'Which one of the following is not a step in merge sort algorithm?', 'Merge sort follows the divide-and-conquer paradigm with three main steps: 1) Divide - recursively split the array into halves until single elements remain, 2) Conquer - sort the base cases (single elements are trivially sorted), 3) Combine - merge sorted sub-arrays back together. \'Analyze\' is not part of the algorithm execution; it\'s what we do to understand algorithm complexity. Merge sort has O(n log n) time complexity in all cases and requires O(n) additional space. It\'s stable (preserves equal elements\' order) and efficient for large datasets.', '2026-03-02 15:00:44'),
(159, 1, 1, 'Software Debugging is known as ________________', 'Debugging specifically refers to locating and fixing defects (bugs) in software code after discovery (usually through testing). It involves systematic diagnosis: reproducing issues, isolating causes, implementing fixes, and verifying solutions. Tools include debuggers, profilers, and log analyzers. Debugging differs from related activities: requirements analysis identifies what to build, coding creates implementation, algorithm design develops solution strategies. Effective debugging requires logical thinking and codebase knowledge. Preventive measures like code reviews, unit testing, and static analysis reduce debugging effort. Mastering debugging improves software reliability and developer efficiency. It\'s a critical skill often consuming significant development time.', '2026-03-02 15:01:10'),
(160, 1, 1, 'Which of the following term refers to the number of symbols composing a string?', 'In formal language theory and computer science, the length of a string is the number of symbols (characters) it contains. For example, the string \'computer\' has length 8. Alphabet refers to the set of allowed symbols, symbols are the individual elements, and word is often synonymous with string. Length is a fundamental property used in algorithms for string processing, validation, and memory allocation. Empty strings have length 0, and length operations typically run in O(1) time in modern programming languages.', '2026-03-02 15:02:16'),
(161, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is correct about symmetric and asymmetric keys?', 'Asymmetric encryption (public-key cryptography) uses key pairs: each participant has a public key (shared openly) and a private key (kept secret). For secure communication, the sender encrypts with the recipient\'s public key, and the recipient decrypts with their private key. Symmetric encryption uses a single shared secret key for both encryption and decryption, requiring secure key distribution. While both can be used in authentication protocols, they are not exclusively authentication methods, and they are certainly not recovery methods.', '2026-03-02 15:03:17'),
(162, 1, 3, 'Which one of the following is false about HTTP protocol?', 'HTTP messages have no minimum size requirement. Requests/responses can be very small (headers only) or large (with body). HTTP is stateless: each request independent, though cookies/sessions simulate state. HTTP/1.1 added persistent connections. Messages consist of: start-line (request/response), headers, empty line, optional body. Common methods: GET (retrieve), POST (submit), PUT (update), DELETE (remove). Status codes: 1xx informational, 2xx success, 3xx redirection, 4xx client error, 5xx server error. HTTP/2 adds multiplexing, compression; HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP.', '2026-03-02 15:04:00'),
(163, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is not an example of divide and conquer algorithm?', 'Heap sort uses a heap data structure (priority queue) and doesn\'t follow the divide-and-conquer pattern of recursively breaking problems into subproblems, solving independently, and combining results. Quick sort (partition then recurse), merge sort (split, sort subarrays, merge), and binary search (halve search space) are classic divide-and-conquer examples. Heap sort builds a max-heap and repeatedly extracts maximum, running in O(n log n) time in-place. Divide-and-conquer algorithms often have recurrence relations solvable by the Master Theorem. Recognizing algorithm paradigms aids in analysis and implementation.', '2026-03-02 15:04:35'),
(164, 1, 3, 'Which of the following access control services determines what a subject can do?', 'Authorization determines access rights after authentication verifies identity. Models: DAC (Discretionary Access Control - owner controls), MAC (Mandatory - system-enforced labels), RBAC (Role-Based - roles/permissions), ABAC (Attribute-Based - policies). Implementation: access control lists (ACLs), capabilities. Authentication proves identity (passwords, biometrics, tokens). Accountability links actions to identities. Audit logs actions for review. Principle of least privilege: minimum necessary access. Access control matrix subjects × objects → rights. Real-world systems combine multiple models for defense in depth.', '2026-03-02 15:05:12'),
(165, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is responsible for controlling the flow of bits between memory and controllers with no need for a constant intervention from the CPU?', 'Which one of the following is responsible for controlling the flow of bits between memory and controllers with no need for a constant intervention from the CPU?', '2026-03-02 15:05:27'),
(166, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is not correct about IP address and DHCP', 'DHCP leases are TEMPORARY, not permanent. Leases have durations (default often 24 hours) after which addresses must be renewed. DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) automates IP configuration (address, subnet mask, gateway, DNS). It uses pools of addresses, dynamically assigning them. Mobility support exists but isn\'t guaranteed—roaming between networks may require reconfiguration. DHCP operations: discover, offer, request, acknowledge (DORA). Understanding lease dynamics explains network behavior: addresses change, conflicts avoided, resources conserved. Reservations can make specific addresses permanent for certain MAC addresses, but standard allocations are temporary.', '2026-03-02 15:06:17'),
(167, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is not correct about logic design?', 'Logical database design is NOT completely independent from physical considerations. While it focuses on data structure without storage details, physical factors often influence logical decisions: performance requirements may denormalize tables, specific DBMS features may dictate design patterns, and access patterns affect relationship representation. Logical design uses data models (ER, relational) and includes normalization, but practical designs balance theoretical purity with implementation realities. The ideal separation exists in theory, but in practice, logical and physical design inform each other iteratively.', '2026-03-02 15:07:19'),
(168, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is a wrong coupling of problem and its environment?', 'Multiple state problems in AI typically involve sequential decision making where the agent must consider future states, often in partially observable or stochastic environments. Describing them as \'deterministic, partially observable\' is inconsistent because deterministic environments are usually fully observable—if everything is determined, observing the current state reveals everything. Partially observable environments often require handling uncertainty, making them non-deterministic in practice. Single state problems (like constraint satisfaction) are fully observable and deterministic, contingency problems handle uncertainty, and exploration problems deal with unknown environments.', '2026-03-02 15:08:11'),
(169, 1, 3, 'In knowledge Representation (KR) how we do represent \'how to drive a car\'?', 'Procedural knowledge represents \'how-to\' knowledge - the sequence of steps, skills, and processes required to perform tasks. Driving a car involves procedures like starting the engine, shifting gears, steering, and braking. Declarative knowledge represents factual information (\'what\' knowledge). Transition knowledge relates to state changes. Information Knowledge is not a standard KR category. In AI systems, procedural knowledge is often represented using production rules, scripts, or procedural networks that capture the step-by-step nature of tasks.', '2026-03-02 15:08:53'),
(170, 1, 1, 'Let KPRIV-A and KPUB-A be private and public keys for Awgichew and KPRIV-M and KPUB-M are private and public keys for Meron respectively. If Awgichew would like to send secured message to Meron, which one of the following scenario is not correct if they are using asymmetric keys?', 'In asymmetric encryption, a sender encrypts with the recipient\'s public key, not their own public key. Option B is incorrect because encrypting with one\'s own public key would only allow decryption with the corresponding private key (which Awgichew has), defeating the purpose of sending to Meron. Proper asymmetric encryption involves: Awgichew encrypts with Meron\'s public key (KPUB-M), Meron decrypts with her private key (KPRIV-M). Option A is also incorrect in practice (one shouldn\'t encrypt with someone else\'s private key), but the question identifies B as the formally incorrect protocol.', '2026-03-02 15:09:23'),
(171, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following cannot affect proper functioning of a system?', 'A UPS (Uninterruptible Power Supply) is designed to protect systems from power issues, not cause them. It provides backup power during outages, regulates voltage, and protects against surges. Improperly placed heaters can cause overheating, humidity can lead to condensation and short circuits, and lightning strikes can cause power surges or electromagnetic interference. A properly functioning UPS enhances system reliability, while the other options represent environmental hazards that can disrupt system operation.', '2026-03-02 15:10:19'),
(172, 1, 3, 'Which programming paradigm emphasizes breaking down problems into smaller, reusable components known as functions or procedures?', 'Procedural programming organizes code into procedures/functions that perform operations on data. It emphasizes step-by-step instructions, modularization, and code reuse through functions. This differs from object-oriented programming (objects with data+methods), functional programming (pure functions, immutability), and imperative programming (broader category including procedural). Languages like C, Pascal, and early versions of BASIC follow procedural paradigms. While effective for many problems, procedural programming can lead to code duplication and maintenance issues compared to OOP\'s encapsulation and inheritance features.', '2026-03-02 15:10:26'),
(173, 1, 1, 'Which one of the following is correct first order logic for the statement \'There is someone standing at the door and is tall\'?', 'The existential quantifier (∃) captures \'there exists someone\', conjunction (^) combines \'standing at door\' and \'tall\'. Option C correctly represents: \'There exists an x such that x is standing at the door AND x is tall\'. Universal quantifier (∀) would mean \'all people\', which is incorrect. Implication (→) in option D would mean \'if someone stands at door then they\'re tall\', which differs from the conjunction. First-order logic precisely represents natural language statements for automated reasoning, with quantifiers and predicates capturing objects, properties, and relations.', '2026-03-02 15:11:30'),
(174, 1, 1, 'If you want to test individual sub-systems of software, which phase of software testing is appropriate?', 'Unit testing focuses on verifying individual components or modules in isolation, typically performed by developers on small units of code like functions, methods, or classes. Integration testing combines and tests multiple units together, system testing evaluates the complete integrated system, and acceptance testing validates against user requirements. Unit tests are fundamental for catching bugs early, enabling refactoring, and ensuring component reliability before integration. They\'re usually automated and run frequently during development.', '2026-03-02 15:12:35'),
(175, 1, 3, 'Which one of the following is a correct statement?', 'Deterministic Finite Automata (DFA) and Nondeterministic Finite Automata (NFA) recognize the same class of languages (regular languages) and can be converted between each other. NFAs may have epsilon transitions and multiple next states, but any NFA can be converted to an equivalent DFA (potentially with state explosion). Turing machines are most powerful (recognize recursively enumerable languages). Pushdown automata are more powerful than FSAs (context-free vs regular). Regular expressions and FSAs are equivalent in expressive power (Kleene\'s theorem).', '2026-03-02 15:15:20'),
(176, 1, 3, 'Brute-force attacks are used most often against which types of access control?', 'Brute-force attacks systematically try all possible combinations against password/PIN systems because these rely on secret knowledge that can be guessed. Passwords (alphanumeric) and PINs (numeric) have limited key spaces making exhaustive search feasible. Countermeasures include: account lockouts, rate limiting, CAPTCHAs, and increasing complexity requirements. Biometrics (fingerprint, facial recognition) aren\'t vulnerable to traditional brute-force as they\'re physical traits. Smart cards use cryptographic keys making brute-force impractical against properly implemented cryptography. Hybrid attacks (dictionary, rainbow tables) enhance brute-force efficiency by trying likely passwords first.', '2026-03-02 15:16:39'),
(177, 1, 3, 'What is the purpose of the switch statement in C++?', 'The switch statement provides multi-way branching based on an expression\'s value. It\'s more efficient than multiple if-else statements when comparing the same variable against constant values. Syntax includes: switch(expression), case constants, break statements (to prevent fall-through), and optional default case. Limitations: expression must be integral or enumeration type; case values must be constants; no range comparisons. Switch improves code readability for menu-driven programs, state machines, and command processors. Modern C++ allows switch on strings through hash conversions and offers enhanced features in newer standards.', '2026-03-02 15:18:01'),
(178, 1, 3, 'What is the term used to describe the collection of information stored in the database at a particular moment?', 'A database instance (or state) represents the actual data content at a specific time. It\'s the collection of data stored in the database following the database schema. The schema is the structural definition (tables, columns, constraints) - the database\'s blueprint. Primary keys uniquely identify rows; foreign keys establish relationships. Instances change through insert, update, delete operations while the schema remains relatively stable. Multiple instances can exist over time (backups, snapshots). Database management systems maintain consistency between the schema and instances through integrity constraints and transactions.', '2026-03-02 15:19:52'),
(179, 1, 3, 'In PHP, which function initiates a session?', 'session_start() creates or resumes a session in PHP. It must be called before any output to the browser. Sessions maintain user state across HTTP requests using session IDs (typically cookies). The function: 1) Checks for existing session ID, 2) Creates new session if none exists, 3) Initializes $_SESSION superglobal array. Session data is stored server-side (files, database, memcached). Alternatives: session_id() for manual control, session_regenerate_id() for security. Sessions end with session_destroy() or timeout. Configuration: session.gc_maxlifetime (lifetime), session.cookie_lifetime (cookie expiry).', '2026-03-02 19:51:45'),
(180, 1, 4, 'Which sorting algorithm is commonly used for small input sizes or partially sorted arrays?', 'Insertion sort efficient for small n (low overhead) and nearly sorted arrays (adaptive). Often used as base case in hybrid sorts like Timsort (used in Python, Java).', '2026-03-02 19:52:48'),
(181, 1, 4, 'Which scheduling algorithm is typically used for non-preemptive scheduling?', 'Non-preemptive scheduling, also known as FCFS scheduling, executes tasks in the order they arrive without interruption. Once a task starts, it runs to completion unless it voluntarily yields the CPU.', '2026-03-02 19:58:58'),
(182, 1, 4, 'Which data structure is suitable for implementing breadth-first search (BFS) algorithm?', 'BFS uses queue to explore neighbors before deeper nodes. Enqueue unvisited neighbors, dequeue current node. Produces level-order traversal. Essential for shortest path in unweighted graphs.', '2026-03-02 20:04:08'),
(183, 1, 4, 'Which algorithm design technique is most suitable for solving problems with exponential time complexity?', 'Backtracking often has exponential time but works for problems with small input sizes where exhaustive search is feasible (e.g., puzzles). Pruning and heuristics can reduce effective complexity.', '2026-03-02 20:05:35'),
(184, 1, 4, 'Which sorting algorithm is known for its stability, meaning that elements with equal keys retain their relative order?', 'Merge sort is stable if merge preserves order of equal elements. Others: insertion sort stable, bubble sort stable, selection sort typically not stable, quick sort usually not stable, heap sort not stable.', '2026-03-02 20:06:53'),
(185, 1, 4, 'Which searching algorithm is suitable for large datasets or databases?', 'Hash search (hashing) provides average O(1) lookup, ideal for large datasets if memory available for hash table. Binary search O(log n) also good but requires sorted data.', '2026-03-02 20:09:10'),
(186, 1, 4, 'Which sorting algorithm has the average-case time complexity of O(n log n)?', 'Quick sort average O(n log n) with good pivot selection. Worst-case O(n^2) but randomized pivot avoids this with high probability. Merge sort always O(n log n) but uses extra space.', '2026-03-02 20:11:51'),
(187, 1, 4, 'Which algorithm design technique can be used to solve problems by combining solutions to subproblems?', 'Divide and conquer\'s combine phase merges subproblem solutions. The key is designing efficient merge operations (e.g., merge sort\'s O(n) merge). Contrast with dynamic programming\'s overlapping subproblems.', '2026-03-02 20:13:40'),
(188, 1, 4, 'Which complexity class includes problems that can be solved by a non-deterministic Turing machine in polynomial time?', 'NP (nondeterministic polynomial time) contains problems verifiable in polynomial time. P ⊆ NP (solving is at least as hard as verifying). NP-complete are hardest in NP; if any NP-complete in P, then P=NP.', '2026-03-02 20:14:48'),
(189, 1, 4, 'Which data structure ensures that elements are accessed in a specific order?', 'Priority queue orders elements by priority (key). Highest/lowest priority element accessed first. Implemented with heap or balanced tree. Not simply FIFO/LIFO but priority-based ordering.', '2026-03-02 20:15:49'),
(190, 1, 4, 'Which data structure is used to implement a buffer with a fixed-size capacity?', 'Circular buffer (ring buffer) uses fixed array with head/tail pointers wrapping around. O(1) enqueue/dequeue, efficient memory use, pre-allocated. Used in streaming, producer-consumer problems.', '2026-03-02 20:17:06'),
(191, 1, 4, 'What is the significance of logarithmic time complexity, such as O(log n)?', 'O(log n) is sublinear: grows slower than linear. Extremely efficient for large n. Achieved by algorithms that repeatedly reduce problem size (binary search, balanced tree operations, binary exponentiation).', '2026-03-02 20:18:29'),
(192, 1, 4, 'Which sorting algorithm has the best average-case time complexity?', 'Quick sort average O(n log n) with small constants, often fastest in practice. Merge sort also O(n log n) but with higher overhead and extra memory. Timsort (adaptive) often better for real data.', '2026-03-02 20:19:33'),
(193, 1, 4, 'What is the process of converting plaintext into ciphertext called?', 'Encryption is the process of converting readable plaintext into unreadable ciphertext using an encryption algorithm and a secret encryption key. Provides confidentiality. Symmetric encryption uses same key (AES, DES). Asymmetric uses key pair (RSA, ECC). Decryption reverses encryption. Hashing one-way (SHA, MD5). Key exchange establishes shared secret (Diffie-Hellman). Understanding encryption protects data at rest and in transit. Algorithm strength depends on key length, design. Modes of operation: ECB, CBC, GCM. Applications: SSL/TLS, disk encryption, messaging. Encryption standards: FIPS, NIST. Quantum computing threatens current algorithms. Proper implementation critical—vulnerabilities in implementation, not just algorithm. Key management essential for security.', '2026-03-02 20:25:14'),
(194, 1, 4, 'Which data structure can be implemented using either an array or a linked list?', 'Stack can use array (fixed/dynamic) or linked list. Array: O(1) push/pop but fixed capacity or resizing cost. Linked list: O(1) push/pop, dynamic but pointer overhead.', '2026-03-02 20:36:19'),
(195, 1, 4, 'Which protocol is used for sending and receiving email messages over the internet?', 'SMTP is the standard protocol used for sending and receiving email messages over the internet. It is responsible for the transfer of email messages between mail servers. SMTP uses port 25 (587 for submission). Email clients use SMTP to send, POP3/IMAP to receive. FTP transfers files, HTTP serves web, DNS resolves names. Understanding email protocols helps email server configuration, troubleshooting, security implementation. SMTP commands: HELO, MAIL FROM, RCPT TO, DATA. Secure variants: SMTPS (port 465), STARTTLS. Email flow: sender → SMTP → recipient\'s SMTP → storage → POP3/IMAP → recipient. Anti-spam measures: SPF, DKIM, DMARC. SMTP relay for forwarding. Authentication: SMTP AUTH.', '2026-03-02 20:37:44'),
(196, 1, 4, 'Which subnet mask is used for a point-to-point link?', 'A point-to-point link only requires two IP addresses, one for each end of the link. Therefore, a subnet mask of 255.255.255.252 is used, which allows for 2 host addresses (1 usable after network/broadcast). /30 mask (255.255.255.252) provides 4 total addresses: network, 2 hosts, broadcast. For point-to-point: often only 2 addresses needed (one each interface). Other masks waste addresses: /24 (254 hosts), /25 (126), /26 (62), /30 efficient. Understanding efficient addressing conserves IP space. Example: 192.168.1.0/30: network 192.168.1.0, hosts 192.168.1.1-2, broadcast 192.168.1.3. Used for router connections, VPN tunnels. IPv6 uses /127 for point-to-point.', '2026-03-02 20:39:06'),
(197, 1, 4, 'Which data structure follows the First-In-First-Out (FIFO) principle?', 'Queue: first element added is first removed. Like line at ticket counter. Used in scheduling, buffering, and BFS graph traversal. Variations: circular queue, priority queue, deque.', '2026-03-02 20:47:34'),
(198, 1, 4, 'What is the role of secure coding practices in program security?', 'Secure coding practices aim to reduce the number of bugs and vulnerabilities in software by following established coding standards, using secure programming techniques, and adopting defensive coding practices. Includes: input validation, output encoding, proper error handling, least privilege, memory safety, cryptographic practices. Doesn\'t primarily address duplication (DRY), compliance (though helps), collaboration (version control). Understanding secure coding prevents vulnerabilities early in SDLC. Resources: OWASP Secure Coding Practices, CERT Secure Coding Standards. Training essential for developers. Tools: static analysis (SAST), dynamic analysis (DAST). Languages with safety features help. Code reviews for security. Shift-left: security early in development. Reduces cost compared to fixing post-deployment. Part of DevSecOps.', '2026-03-02 20:49:39'),
(199, 1, 4, 'Which type of vulnerability occurs when software developers unintentionally expose sensitive infor', 'Information disclosure vulnerabilities occur when an application reveals sensitive data such as system details, database schemas, file paths, or user information through error messages, logs, or responses. This information can aid attackers in further exploits. For example, SQL error messages might reveal database structure. Prevention includes generic error messages in production, proper logging without sensitive data, and input validation. This differs from buffer overflow (memory corruption), XSS (client-side scripting), or privilege escalation (rights elevation). Information disclosure is part of the CIA triad (Confidentiality) and is addressed through secure coding, configuration management, and security testing.', '2026-03-02 20:50:45'),
(200, 1, 4, 'Which recovery method involves using a combination of checkpoint information and transaction logs to restore the database?', 'Restart recovery uses checkpoint data as starting points and processes logs forward (redo) and backward (undo) to restore consistency. It\'s the standard approach for crash recovery in most DBMS.', '2026-03-02 20:51:50'),
(201, 1, 4, 'How many subnets can be created from a Class B network with a subnet mask of 255.255.240.0?', 'With a subnet mask of 255.255.240.0, there are 4 subnet bits (in third octet: 240=11110000). The formula to calculate the number of subnets is 2^(number of subnet bits) = 2^4 = 16 subnets. Class B default mask 255.255.0.0 (/16). 255.255.240.0 = /20 (16+4). Subnet bits borrowed from host portion. Understanding subnetting helps network segmentation, security, management. For Class B: 16 network bits, 16 host bits originally. With /20: 4 subnet bits, 12 host bits per subnet. Hosts per subnet: 2^12-2=4094. Subnetting creates smaller broadcast domains, improves performance, applies policies. Example: 172.16.0.0/20 subnets: 172.16.0.0, 172.16.16.0, ..., 172.16.240.0.', '2026-03-02 20:53:15'),
(202, 1, 4, 'What is the difference between static typing and dynamic typing in programming?', 'Static typing (C++, Java) checks types at compile-time; dynamic typing (Python, JavaScript) checks at runtime. Static typing catches errors earlier but is less flexible; dynamic typing enables rapid prototyping but risks runtime type errors.', '2026-03-02 20:54:23'),
(203, 1, 4, 'What is the purpose of responsive web design?', 'Responsive web design uses flexible layouts, images, and CSS media queries to ensure websites work well on various devices (desktops, tablets, phones). It improves user experience and SEO across screen sizes.', '2026-03-02 20:55:53'),
(204, 1, 4, 'When designing complex software systems with multiple interacting objects, what design principle is crucial for maintainability and scalability?', 'Loose coupling minimizes dependencies between components, allowing changes in one part with minimal impact on others. This is achieved through interfaces, dependency injection, and design patterns. It improves maintainability, testability, and scalability. Tight coupling and global variables create hidden dependencies and side effects, making systems brittle. Code duplication violates DRY (Don\'t Repeat Yourself). Understanding coupling and cohesion is fundamental to software architecture, enabling modular, reusable, and adaptable designs. Principles like SOLID, design patterns, and modular programming promote loose coupling in large-scale software development.', '2026-03-02 21:02:40'),
(205, 1, 4, 'What is the role of a security administrator in system administration?', 'Security administrators are responsible for enforcing security policies and procedures, implementing security controls, and addressing security threats and vulnerabilities. Specialized security focus. Network monitoring by network admins. User accounts by sysadmins. Network device configuration by network admins. Understanding security admin role. Responsibilities: risk assessment, security architecture, incident response, compliance, awareness training. Tools: SIEM, vulnerability scanners, firewalls, IDS/IPS. Policies: acceptable use, password, incident response. Collaboration with all IT teams. Compliance with regulations. Security monitoring and analysis. Incident investigation. Security assessments. Patch management coordination. Security awareness programs. Certifications: CISSP, Security+, CISM. Growing importance with increasing threats. Part of cybersecurity team.', '2026-03-02 21:03:39'),
(206, 1, 4, 'What is the purpose of a framework in web development?', 'Web frameworks (Django, Ruby on Rails, Laravel) provide pre-built components, patterns, and tools to streamline development. They handle common tasks like routing, database interaction, and security, promoting best practices.', '2026-03-02 21:04:34'),
(207, 1, 4, 'Which sorting algorithm works by repeatedly selecting the smallest element from the unsorted portion and placing it at the beginning?', 'Selection sort: find minimum in unsorted part, swap with first unsorted position. O(n^2) always, not stable, minimal swaps (O(n)). Inefficient but simple.', '2026-03-02 21:05:31'),
(208, 1, 4, 'Which algorithm is commonly used for finding the shortest path in a graph?', 'Dijkstra\'s algorithm is widely used for finding the shortest path between nodes in a weighted graph with non-negative edge weights. It uses a greedy approach, maintaining a priority queue of vertices with their current shortest distance from the source. BFS finds shortest path in unweighted graphs. DFS explores deeply, not necessarily shortest. Prim\'s finds minimum spanning tree. Dijkstra\'s time complexity is O((V+E) log V) with binary heap. Applications: routing protocols (OSPF), navigation systems, network analysis. Understanding graph algorithms helps solve optimization problems in networks, transportation, and social networks. Dijkstra\'s cannot handle negative weights (use Bellman-Ford). Variants: A* for heuristics.', '2026-03-02 21:06:30'),
(209, 1, 4, 'Which recovery method is typically faster but requires additional storage space?', 'Backup recovery is faster than rebuilding from logs but requires significant storage for backup copies. Incremental backups reduce storage but complicate restoration compared to full backups.', '2026-03-02 21:08:25'),
(210, 1, 4, 'Which normal form is achieved when all non-key attributes are dependent on the primary key, the whole primary key, and nothing but the primary key?', 'BCNF is stricter than 3NF: every determinant must be a candidate key. It eliminates all redundancy from functional dependencies but may not preserve all dependencies (requires decomposition that might lose some FDs).', '2026-03-02 21:09:33'),
(211, 1, 4, 'What is a race condition vulnerability?', 'A race condition vulnerability occurs when the outcome of a program depends on the relative timing of different threads or processes, potentially leading to unexpected or insecure behavior. Common in concurrent systems. Example: TOCTTOU (Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use). Can cause security bypass, privilege escalation, data corruption. Prevention: synchronization (mutex, semaphore), atomic operations, immutable data. Understanding race conditions important for multi-threaded programming. Difficult to reproduce, detect. Testing: stress testing, static analysis tools. Languages with concurrency primitives help. File system race conditions common. Impact: unauthorized access, denial of service. Defense: secure coding practices for concurrency. Real-world examples: symlink attacks. Part of software security assessment.', '2026-03-02 21:10:27'),
(212, 1, 4, 'Which object-oriented concept is used to create objects based on a predefined blueprint or class?', 'Instantiation creates objects from classes using new operator. Objects are instances with their own state (field values) while sharing behavior (methods) defined in class. Memory allocated on heap.', '2026-03-02 21:11:27'),
(213, 1, 4, 'Which layer of the OSI reference model is responsible for reliable data delivery within the same network segment?', 'The Data Link layer is responsible for reliable data delivery within the same network segment. It handles the framing of data packets, error detection, and correction at the link level. OSI Layer 2. Functions: physical addressing (MAC), framing, error control, flow control, access control. Devices: switches, bridges. Network layer handles routing across networks, Transport provides end-to-end reliability, Application interfaces with users. Understanding Data Link helps LAN design, switch configuration, error detection. Protocols: Ethernet, Wi-Fi (802.11), PPP. Sublayers: LLC (Logical Link Control), MAC (Media Access Control). Error detection: CRC. Flow control: sliding window. MAC addressing ensures delivery to correct device within broadcast domain.', '2026-03-02 21:12:33'),
(214, 1, 4, 'Which sorting algorithm has the worst-case time complexity of O(n^2)?', 'Bubble sort worst-case O(n^2) (reverse sorted). Actually all except merge/heap have worst-case O(n^2). But bubble sort is classic example of inefficient O(n^2) sort.', '2026-03-02 21:13:36'),
(215, 1, 4, 'What is the purpose of version control systems (VCS) in web development?', 'Version control systems (Git, SVN) track code changes, enable collaboration through branching/merging, maintain history, and facilitate rollbacks. They\'re essential for team-based web development and deployment workflows.', '2026-03-02 21:14:29'),
(216, 1, 4, 'Which of the following is a common authentication factor used in two-factor authentication?', 'Two-factor authentication combines two different authentication factors, such as a password and a one-time password (OTP) generated by a mobile app or sent via SMS, to provide an additional layer of security. Authentication factors: knowledge (password), possession (token, phone), inherence (biometric). OTP is possession factor. Username identifies, password is single factor, security question is knowledge. Understanding 2FA/MFA improves account security. OTP methods: time-based (TOTP), HMAC-based (HOTP), SMS-based (less secure). Applications: online banking, email, VPN. 2FA prevents account takeover even if password compromised. Implementation: hardware tokens, authenticator apps, biometrics. Standards: RFC 6238 (TOTP). Phishing-resistant 2FA: FIDO2/WebAuthn. Balance security with usability.', '2026-03-02 21:15:28'),
(217, 1, 4, 'What is data replication in a distributed database system?', 'Replication maintains copies at multiple sites for availability, reliability, and performance. Synchronous replication ensures strong consistency but impacts performance; asynchronous replication offers better performance with eventual consistency.', '2026-03-02 21:16:20'),
(218, 1, 4, 'A company wants to track employee performance by department and job title. Which SQL operation would be used to accomplish this task?', 'GROUP BY aggregates data by specified columns (e.g., department, job_title) allowing calculations like averages, counts, or sums per group. Combined with aggregate functions (COUNT, AVG, SUM), it enables performance analysis. SELECT retrieves columns. FROM specifies tables. JOIN combines tables. GROUP BY is essential for summary reports and analytical queries in SQL. Understanding aggregation and grouping is crucial for business intelligence, data analysis, and generating insights from relational databases.', '2026-03-02 21:17:04'),
(219, 1, 4, 'Which of the following is the correct syntax to define a function in C++?', 'Function definition requires return type, name, parameters, and body. void functions don\'t return values but can use return; to exit early. Functions returning values must have return statement with compatible type.', '2026-03-02 21:18:16'),
(220, 1, 4, 'Which data structure can be easily implemented using an array?', 'Stack with array: maintain top index, push increments, pop decrements. Simple and cache-friendly. Queue with array requires circular buffer or shifting. Linked list and tree require pointers.', '2026-03-02 21:19:21'),
(221, 1, 4, 'Which database model represents data as a collection of tables with rows and columns?', 'Relational model organizes data into tables (relations) with rows (tuples) and columns (attributes). It uses mathematical set theory and relational algebra, providing declarative SQL for data manipulation.', '2026-03-02 21:20:27'),
(222, 1, 4, 'What is the role of the sizeof operator in C++?', 'sizeof is a compile-time operator returning size of type or expression. Useful for dynamic memory allocation (malloc(sizeof(type)*n)), array bounds checking, and understanding memory layout.', '2026-03-02 21:21:41'),
(223, 1, 4, 'Which data structure is best suited for implementing a priority queue?', 'Binary heap provides O(log n) insert/extract-max(min), O(1) peek. Balanced BST also O(log n) but more overhead. Array/linked list would be O(n) for one operation.', '2026-03-02 21:22:40'),
(224, 1, 4, 'What is the purpose of version control systems in software development?', 'Version control systems (VCS) track changes to source code and files over time. They enable collaboration, maintain history, support branching/merging, and facilitate rollback to previous states. Examples: Git, SVN, Mercurial.', '2026-03-02 21:24:03');
